var e=[{tag:`e001`,title:`The Adventure Begins`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`Now consult r202 to determine your starting attributes (r201) as Cal Arath, the Barbarian Prince, and then return to this event. To escape the mercenary royal guard, your loyal servant Ogab has smuggled you into a merchant caravan to the southern border. Roll 1D6 to see where the caravan takes you (while poor Ogab faces the hostile royal guards back home): 1: Ogon (hex 0101); 2: hex 0701; 3: Ruins of Jakor's Keep (hex 0901); 4: hex 1301; 5: Weshor (hex 1501); 6: hex 1801. Now, as dawn rises, you roll out of the merchant wagons into a ditch. You stop to study a scroll that Ogab has given you (see e399, then return here), dust off your clothes, loosen your`,note:`If you finish actions for a day on a hex north of the Tragoth River, then after any normal events are concluded, but before you take your evening meal (r215), roll 1D6 (-1 to roll if in Jakor’s Keep, +1 to roll if in Ogon or Weshor): 0: e045; 1-3: No event; 4-7: Mercenary royal guards find you - see e002.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Starting Location (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Ogon (hex 0101)`],[`2`,`Hex 0701`],[`3`,`Ruins of Jakor's Keep (hex 0901)`],[`4`,`Hex 1301`],[`5`,`Weshor (hex 1501)`],[`6`,`Hex 1801`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`North of Tragoth River (evening, 1D6, -1 in Jakor's Keep, +1 in Ogon/Weshor)`,rows:[[`0`,`See e045`],[`1–3`,`No event`],[`4–7`,`Mercenary royal guards find you – see e002`]]}]},{tag:`e002`,title:`Mercenary Royal Guardsmen`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Mercenary thugs, dressed by the Northern usurpers as their royal guardsmen, are riding swiftly on horses towards you! Roll 1D6+1 (1D3+1 if this event takes place during the first week of your adventure) for the number of men (each is S5, E4, W4), and then roll another 1D6:`,note:`If you encounter Ogab or Volk after they have been already killed in the game, encounter them again as normal. Strange and dark magic must be at work...`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Special Condition (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`(Ψ) Led by Volk (†S8, E8, W30). +2 men, -1 to encounter roll. Kill Volk → only 300 gold needed to win.`],[`2–5`,`No additional event`],[`6`,`(Ψ) Guards drag Ogab (S4, E4, E2 currently, W0). If freed he joins as ally (r334), +1 Wit & Wiles.`]]},{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Negotiate`,`Evade*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`pass r327`,`attacked r307`,`surprise r300`],[`2`,`pass r328`,`attack r306`,`surprise r301`],[`3`,`pass r329`,`attack r304`,`surprise r303`],[`4`,`bribe to pass (15) r323`,`hide r318`,`attack r304`],[`5`,`bribe to pass (25) r323`,`escape r311`,`attack r305`],[`6+`,`attacked r306`,`escape mounted r312`,`attack r306`]]}]},{tag:`e003`,title:`Swordsman`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`You meet an intriguing swordsman adventurer (†S6, E6, W7) on horseback. On 5–6, the adventurer is a swordswoman.`,note:`Add +1 W&W for Talk. If joined: on 6 they have news (see nearest habitation event). Swordswoman: on 6, she becomes your lover (r228). Flying party may use r313 for evade.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`ally r334`,`escape mounted r312`,`surprise r303`],[`2`,`converse r341`,`escape r315`,`attack r304`],[`3`,`looter r340`,`hide r317`,`attack r305`],[`4`,`hireling r339`,`bribe (5) r322`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`hireling r333`,`pass r325`,`attacked r307`],[`6`,`bribe (10) r332`,`pass r325`,`surprised r308`]]}]},{tag:`e004`,title:`Mercenary Band`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A small mercenary band approaches. Leader (†S6, E6, W50) mounted. Roll 1D6 for number of troopers (each S5, E4, W4). 1–2 troopers are mounted, otherwise on foot.`,note:`Roll 1D6 first — on 1 (1–3 north of Tragoth) see e002 instead. Talk: may pay 10 gold bribe (r322) to dismiss. Mounts/flying give evade bonuses.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`conversation r341`,`hide r319`,`surprise r301`],[`2`,`bribe to hire (20) r332`,`pass r328`,`surprise r302`],[`3`,`hirelings r338`,`pass r329`,`surprise r303`],[`4`,`hirelings r339`,`hide r317`,`attack r304`],[`5`,`looters r340`,`pass r327`,`attack r305`],[`6+`,`attacked r306`,`escape mounted r312`,`attack r306`]]}]},{tag:`e005`,title:`Amazons`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A group of 1D3+1 tough Amazon warriors (each S6, E5, W4) approach. They are blood-sisters — hire, serve, and desert together. Never suffer more than -1 combat penalty when wounded.`,note:`If Amazons join: on 6, one becomes your lover (r228). Mounts allow escape mounted (r312) instead.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`conversation r341`,`escape r311`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`hirelings r338`,`escape r314`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`hirelings r339`,`escape r315`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`looters r340`,`hide r319`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`pass r327`,`pass r328`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`attacked r306`,`hide r317`,`battle r330`]]}]},{tag:`e006`,title:`Dwarf`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a Dwarf warrior (†S6, E7, W21) with a long beard and large axe. Roll 1D6 to determine if alone or accompanied.`,note:`Add +1 to encounter roll if dwarf is alone. If joined: acts as mountain guide (r205a), reveals ancient Mine location. Mounts allow escape mounted (r312).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Dwarf Situation (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`Dwarf travelling alone`],[`3`,`Dwarf is a merchant — see e128`],[`4`,`Dwarf has one companion (S5, E6, W12)`],[`5`,`Dwarf has 1D6 dwarves with him (each S5, E6, W12)`],[`6`,`Dwarf leads a band — see e058`]]},{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6, +1 if alone)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`surprised r308`,`bribe (5) r322`,`surprised r308`],[`2`,`bribe to hire (10) r332`,`pass r328`,`attacked r306`],[`3`,`looter r340`,`escape r314`,`attacked r306`],[`4`,`hirelings r339`,`escape r315`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`hirelings r333`,`escape r311`,`attack r305`],[`6`,`*conversation r341`,`attacked r306`,`attack r304`],[`7`,`*ally r334`,`escape r311`,`surprise r303`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Ancient Mine Discovery (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`e059`],[`3–4`,`e067`],[`5`,`e064`],[`6`,`e028`]]}]},{tag:`e007`,title:`Elf`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a wise Elf (†S5, E5, W15). On 5–6 the Elf is female. Roll 1D6 for additional characteristics. All Elves gain +1S in ranged combat. Subtract -1 W&W in this event.`,note:`Add +2 to roll in forest hex. If joined: +1 W&W, forest guide (r205a). Female elf: on 6, becomes your lover (r228). Non-forest: may use escape mounted (r312).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Elf Characteristics (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Travelling alone`],[`2`,`Leader of an elf band — see e071`],[`3`,`Magician with death mist potion (e190); uses it on surprise attack (r220d)`],[`4`,`Priest of forest cult with healing potion (e180)`],[`5`,`Magician with cure poison vial (e181) and an apprentice (S3, E3, W2)`],[`6`,`Has a companion elf (S4, E4, W7)`]]},{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6, +2 in forest)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`hide r317`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`conversation r341`,`hide r318`,`attack r305`],[`3`,`inquiry r342`,`escape r314`,`attacked r306`],[`4`,`ally r334`,`escape r315`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`escapee r335`,`escape r315`,`surprised r308`],[`6`,`plead comrades r336`,`pass r326`,`battle r330`],[`7`,`pass r328`,`pass r329`,`surprised r309`],[`8`,`pass r329`,`surprised r310`,`surprised r310`]]}]},{tag:`e008`,title:`Halfling`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a stout halfling (S3 / S4 ranged, E6, W4). You may attack, talk, or pass. In battle, after each round on 2–6 the halfling escapes. Killing him in a halfling village marks you as 'wanted'.`,note:`Talking ends all further travel today. Giving food allows rolling twice and choosing result.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Talk Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Banal conversation — time wasted`],[`2`,`Tells location of his village in adjacent hex (→ e070). If already there, taken to favourite tavern (r230)`],[`3`,`General inquiries — see r342 (+2 to roll)`],[`4–5`,`Extended conversation — see e341 (+2 to roll)`],[`6`,`Choice gossip: 1–3 → e147; 4–6 → e162`]]}]},{tag:`e009`,title:`Farm`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You spot a small farm. Detour around it (ends travel) or approach with a friendly or raid intent. May preview farm type by rolling under W&W on 1D6 before deciding.`,note:`After a raid, if you remain in the hex, roll 1D6 next morning — on 5+ see e017 (vengeful mob).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Farm Type (2D6)`,rows:[[`2–3`,`e012 – Farmer with Protector`],[`4`,`e011 – Peaceful Farmer`],[`5`,`e014 – Hostile Reaver Clan`],[`6`,`e010 – Starving Farmer`],[`7`,`e011 – Peaceful Farmer`],[`8`,`e013 – Rich Peasant Family`],[`9`,`e015 – Friendly Reaver Clan`],[`10`,`e012 – Farmer with Protector`],[`11+`,`e016 – Magician's Home`]]}]},{tag:`e010`,title:`Starving Farmer`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`The farmer's harvest has failed and his family is starving.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach Type`,rows:[[`Friendly — Refuse charity (if able)`,`All followers roll 1D6 next morning — desert on 3+`],[`Friendly — Grant charity or cannot give`,`No further event`],[`Raid`,`Family quickly killed, nothing gained (farmer was starving)`]]}]},{tag:`e011`,title:`Peaceful Farmer`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`The farmer is generous and welcoming.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach Type`,rows:[[`Friendly`,`Free food & lodging tonight. Sell food at 4 units/gold. Work alone for 1 gold/day + free food & lodging (r233). On leaving: roll 1D6, on 6 farmer's son joins (S3, E4, W2, guide within 2 hexes).`],[`Raid`,`Family fights back (S4, E7, W4) — see r330. If all killed: gain 1D6×4 food units.`]]}]},{tag:`e012`,title:`Farmer with Protector`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`The farmer warns you off but offers to sell food. If raided, sends smoke signals and a Protector (S6, E5, W25) arrives with 4 men (each S5, E5, W4).`,note:`If all opponents killed in raid: find hidden treasure — see e040.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach Type`,rows:[[`Friendly`,`Sells food at 2 units/gold in any quantity. Event ends.`],[`Raid`,`Family (S4, E7, W7) — party strikes first. After each round roll 1D6: on 3+ Protector & 4 men arrive and strike first next round.`]]}]},{tag:`e013`,title:`Rich Peasant Family`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`A prosperous family provides food and lodging at town rates. Roll 1D6: on 4–6 they have stables with horses for sale (roll 1D6 for number, 2D6 gold each). Sell food at 2 units/gold.`,note:`Roll 1D6: on 4–6 may work on farm for 1 gold/day + free food & lodging.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach Type`,rows:[[`Friendly`,`Food & lodging at town rates (r215/r217). Stables on 4–6. May work farm on 4–6.`],[`Raid`,`Fight 4 retainers (S4, E4, W1) — r306. If killed, fight family (S5, E6, W30) — r305. If all dead: gain 1D6×6 food + 1D3 horses.`]]}]},{tag:`e014`,title:`Hostile Reaver Clan`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`Roll 1D6+2 for clan members. Leader S5, E5, W10; others S4, E4, W4.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach Type`,rows:[[`Friendly — clan ≥ party size`,`Surprise attack — r307`],[`Friendly — party > clan`,`Bar house, bid you pass. May make general inquiries (r342) — non-joining reavers auto surprise attack (r307); joiners don't participate.`],[`Raid`,`Battle — r330`]]}]},{tag:`e015`,title:`Friendly Reaver Clan`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`Roll 1D6+1 for clan members. Leader S5, E4, W7; others S4, E4, W4. Leader discusses terms (r342). Sells food at 2 units/gold and horses at 6 gold each regardless of outcome.`,note:`Roll 1D6: on 4–6 may work farm for 1 gold/day + free food & lodging.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach Type`,rows:[[`Friendly`,`Discuss terms (r342). Sells food 2/gold, horses 6 gold. Work farm on 4–6.`],[`Raid`,`Battle — r330. Gain 2 horses if victorious.`]]}]},{tag:`e016`,title:`Magician's Home`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`A magician (S3, E5, W25; female on 5–6) insists you stay the night. Provide your own food. May attempt to recruit (r342). If no combat: receive magic gift (Treasure Table B, r226).`,note:`If joined and not attacking in a round: may enchant all party weapons +1S until end of round.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach Type`,rows:[[`Friendly`,`Stay the night, tell adventures. Try to recruit (r342). If avoided or no combat: magic gift (Treasure Table B).`],[`Raid`,`Magician uses powers — all followers die/flee unless your own magician/wizard/witch blocks (roll 4–6). Take 1D6 wounds (1 poisoned). May escape (r318) or attack (r304). Resistance Talisman (e184) can stop powers. Kill magician: wealth 1=5, 2–3=25, 4–5=60, 6=110.`]]}]},{tag:`e017`,title:`Peasant Mob in Hot Pursuit`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`A large angry mob traps you. Roll 2D6×2 for number of men. Leader S3, E2, W2; all others S2, E2, W1. Must fight your way out — see r330.`},{tag:`e018`,title:`Priest`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A local priest (S3, E3, W25; priestess on 5–6) rides on a donkey. Seems aloof but may be afraid. May let him pass or choose an option.`,note:`If killed: on 5–6 he curses you with 'Unholy Mark' — roll for each follower, desert if roll > W&W. All monks/priests refuse to join. No audience (r211) at marked temples. Remove by Offering (r212) rolling 9+. If joined: magical/undead -1S vs party, heals 1 wound/day, +1 to Offering rolls (r212).`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`plead comrades r336`,`surprise r301`],[`2`,`plead comrades r336`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`plead comrades r337`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`conversation r341`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`inquiry r342`,`attack r305`],[`6`,`pass r325`,`attack r306`]]}]},{tag:`e019`,title:`Hermit Monk`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A hermit monk (S3, E6, W0) meditates quietly in the wilderness, ignoring you.`,note:`Without a priest/monk in party: -1 W&W for this event. On evade roll 6 'monk': hermit is not who he appears — roll 1D6: 1–3 → e020; 4–6 → e021.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`pass r325`,`attack r305`],[`2`,`conversation r341`,`pass r325`,`attack r305`],[`3`,`*plead comrades r336`,`pass r325`,`attacked r306`],[`4`,`*plead comrades r336`,`pass r326`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`pass r325`,`pass r326`,`attacked r307`],[`6`,`pass r325`,`monk**`,`surprised r308`]]}]},{tag:`e020`,title:`Traveling Monk`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A travelling monk (S4, E5, W4) is eager to talk. On 5–6 he is accompanied by another monk of equal abilities. Add +1 W&W to Talk rolls as the monk is eager to help.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`conversation r341`,`pass r325`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`conversation r341`,`pass r325`,`attack r304`],[`3`,`inquiry r342`,`pass r326`,`attack r305`],[`4`,`plead comrades r336`,`inquiry r342`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`plead comrades r337`,`inquiry r342`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`surprised r309`,`escape r311`,`attacked r306`]]}]},{tag:`e021`,title:`Warrior Monks`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A party of grim-faced warrior monks (each S6, E6, W10) with weapons and armour. Roll 2D6 and halve (round up) for number. On 4+ they are all mounted on horses.`,note:`If party has mounts and monks do not: may use escape mounted (r312). If party can fly: may use escape flying (r313).`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`conversation r341`,`pass r325`,`surprise r301`],[`2`,`inquiry r342`,`hide r317`,`surprise r302`],[`3`,`pass r329`,`hide r319`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`bribe to pass (10) r323`,`escape r315`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`attacked r306`,`bribe (10) r324`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`attacked r306`,`pass r329`,`attacked r306`]]}]},{tag:`e022`,title:`Druid`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A druid (†S4, E4, W4), usually found in forest. On 6, the druid is female. Ignores you unless you approach.`,note:`If joined: forest guide (r205a), forages own food (r215b), may calm wolves/boar/bear/hunting cat (5–6 on 1D6). Leaves if entering town/castle/dwarf mine (except Elf Haven). In combat may use lightning bolt instead of normal strike (5–6 on 1D6; roll 1D6 wounds; auto-escapes if wounded when bolt strikes).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Herbs (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`None`],[`3–5`,`e180 – Healing Potion`],[`6`,`e181 – Cure Poison Vial`]]},{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Fight**`],rows:[[`1`,`plead comrades r336`,`surprise r301`],[`2`,`plead comrades r336`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`plead comrades r337`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`conversation r341`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`inquiry r342`,`attack r305`],[`6`,`pass r325`,`attack r306`]]}]},{tag:`e023`,title:`Wizard`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A wizard (†S4, E4, W60) with a henchman (S5, E4, W4). On 3–6 both are mounted. On 1, the wizard is a Dark Wizard — see e024 (Ψ). In combat may use fireball spells (e023a).`,note:`If joined: wizard reveals treasure location — roll 1D6 direction and distance in hexes, then see e026. Roll 3 on talk or 4 on any column triggers e024.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`pass r325`,`attack r305`],[`2`,`inquiry r342`,`pass r325`,`attacked r306`],[`3`,`conversation r341`,`bribe-pass (5) r321`,`see e024`],[`4`,`see e024`,`escape r314`,`see e024`],[`5`,`pass r328`,`escape r313`,`surprised r308`],[`6+`,`ally r334`,`see e024`,`attacked r307`]]}]},{tag:`e023a`,title:`Wizard Fireballs`,type:`magic`,image:!1,desc:`In combat (r220), the wizard may use fireballs, instead of making a normal strike. Before each strike by the wizard, roll 1D6. If the result is 5-6, he strikes with a fireball spell. When the spell strikes, roll 1D3 for the number of wounds each character in the opposing party suffers (your party if in combat against the wizard). If the wizard is striking against you and has been wounded, he automatically escapes when the fireball strikes. Otherwise he remains and the battle continues.`},{tag:`e024`,title:`Wizard Attack`,type:`magic`,image:!1,desc:`The wizard attempts to enslave you and your party. Roll 1D6; if result exceeds W&W (equals or exceeds if Dark Wizard), all followers become slaves and lose wealth/mounts. If failed, party may escape (r218) or attack (r305). Wizard may use fireballs (e023a).`,note:`If enslaved, roll again — if result exceeds W&W, you too are captured. Lose all possessions. Each day: random direction travel, 1D6 direction, on 1 reach tower (e068a) → dungeon (e062). Every 3 days enslaved: 1 wound. End of each day: roll 1D6 — on 6 escape (r218), on 1 caught (+1 wound).`},{tag:`e025`,title:`Wizard's Apprentice`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`The wizard's young apprentice (S3, E4, W7) wants to leave the tower. Will join freely, brings a magic item (Treasure Table B, r226) and may know treasure clues (e026, -1 to roll). Acts as wizard on 5–6 (1D6).`,note:`If you let him join, must escape tower (r218a). Roll 1D6 — if higher than W&W, wizard sends fireballs after you (e023). Cannot return to Wizard's Tower.`},{tag:`e026`,title:`Search for Treasure`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You believe you have found the location of a treasure. Spend one day searching like a cache (r214), then roll 1D6 for result.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Search Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Bogus information — nothing found`],[`2`,`e027 – Ancient Treasure`],[`3`,`e028 – Cave Tombs`],[`4`,`e029 – Danger & Treasure`],[`5–6`,`Clues point to a random adjacent hex — apply this event again on arrival`]]}]},{tag:`e027`,title:`Ancient Treasure`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You find an ancient long-lost treasure (r225), wealth 110.`,note:`Roll 1D6: on 1–2 it is protected by a trap (r227, applied to a random character — r343).`},{tag:`e028`,title:`Cave Tombs`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`Amid howling winds on a craggy cliff you find caves containing the tombs of an ancient race. You may leave or venture inward. If a dwarf is with you, roll twice and choose either result.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Inner Tombs (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e030 – Mummies`],[`2`,`e031 – Looted Tomb`],[`3`,`e032 – Ghosts`],[`4`,`e033 – Warrior Wraiths`],[`5`,`e034 – Spectre of the Inner Tomb`],[`6`,`e029 – Danger & Treasure`]]}]},{tag:`e029`,title:`Danger & Treasure`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`Exploring dark tunnels you sense both danger and treasure nearby. Roll 1D6 (twice and choose if a dwarf is with you).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e040 – Trapped Chest`],[`2`,`e032 – Ghosts`],[`3`,`e036 – Golem at the Gate`],[`4`,`e037 – Broken Chest`],[`5`,`e038 – Cache under Stone`],[`6`,`e044 – High Altar`]]}]},{tag:`e030`,title:`Mummies`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`You discover dried mummies of a long lost race. Among the dust you find just 1 gold piece. In a desert hex, roll 1D6 — on 4–6 the mummies arise and attack (r308). There are 1D3 mummies (each S5, E4*) that inflict only poison wounds (r222) due to their rotting flesh.`},{tag:`e031`,title:`Looted Tomb`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You find the partially looted tomb of a long dead prince. Under his sarcophagus is a secret compartment with a trap lock. Roll 1D6 — if W&W exceeds roll, you outwit the trap; otherwise see r227. Either way you find wealth 50 and a Gift of Charm (e182).`,note:`The Gift of Charm is very old — roll 1D6, on 5–6 it turns to dust and is worthless.`},{tag:`e032`,title:`Ghosts`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Terrifying shades suddenly appear and surprise you in combat (r220). Roll 1D6+1 for number of ghosts (each S4 / S3 with priest, E2*). They guard an ancient altar.`,note:`If all ghosts killed, may investigate the altar — roll 1D6: 1→e037; 2→e039; 3→e041; 4→e042; 5→e044; 6→crumbling relic, no event.`},{tag:`e033`,title:`Warrior Wraiths`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`Hooded wraiths of long dead warriors assault your party and strike first (r220). Roll 1D6 for number (minimum 2, a roll of 1 counts as 2). Each Wraith S6 (S5 with priest), E8*. Guard plunder from an ancient battle.`,note:`If all killed: roll 1D6 for wealth code — 1:25; 2:50; 3:60; 4:70; 5–6:100.`},{tag:`e034`,title:`Spectre of the Inner Tomb`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`A hidden passage leads to a long hall taking the rest of the day to explore. You sense a restless spectre. May retreat now or continue. At end of day: roll 1D6, on 6 see e200, otherwise Spectre S7 (S6 with priest), E3*. Only hurt by poison wounds, magic sword, or attacks from priest/monk/witch/magician/wizard.`,note:`If spectre killed: roll 1D6 for wealth (r225) — 1:5; 2:12; 3–4:25; 5:60; 6:110.`},{tag:`e035`,title:`Spell of Chaos`,type:`magic`,image:!0,desc:`A guardian spell of chaos activates. Wizard/magician/witch in party: roll 1D6 — on 4–6 they protect the party and the spell is harmless. Otherwise your entire party becomes mindless idiots, losing all followers and possessions except your own mount and personally carried wealth.`,note:`Next morning roll 1D6 -1 for days you remain mindless and wander randomly (1 hex/day, no travel events, no eating). When mind returns, resume normally next day.`},{tag:`e036`,title:`Golem at the Gate`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`Separated from your party, you pass through a ruined temple gateway. A huge stone golem (†S6, E8*, W0) strikes first in combat (r220). Cannot escape (r218) — must fight.`,note:`If golem killed: roll 1D6 for next event — 1:e038; 2:e040; 3:e043; 4:e044; 5:e046; 6:e027. If you escape combat (r220e): roll 1D6, if higher than W&W you lose all followers and mounts. If you defeat golem and survive without escaping (r218): rejoin party at evening meal.`},{tag:`e037`,title:`Broken Chest`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You find an old chest with a broken and cracked lid. Sorting through old mouldering cloths, roll 1D6 to see what you find.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Contents (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e180 – Healing Potion`],[`2`,`e181 – Cure Poison Vial`],[`3`,`e182 – Gift of Charm`],[`4`,`e184 – Resistance Talisman`],[`5`,`e186 – Magic Sword`],[`6`,`e189 – Charisma Talisman`]]}]},{tag:`e038`,title:`Cache under Stone`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You overturn a stone slab uncovering an old cache of rotting food and other items. Roll 1D6 to see what you find.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Contents (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e180 – Healing Potion`],[`2`,`e181 – Cure Poison Vial`],[`3`,`e182 – Gift of Charm`],[`4`,`e185 – Poison Drug`],[`5`,`e187 – Anti-Poison Amulet`],[`6`,`e190 – Death Mist Potion`]]}]},{tag:`e039`,title:`Treasure Chest`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You find a locked chest that may be trapped. Roll 1D6 — on 3–6 it has a trap lock (r227). If survived and opened, roll 1D6 — on 6 the chest is empty, otherwise wealth 60.`,note:`If a Pegasus winged mount (e188) would be found: instead roll 1D6 and consult Treasure Table A (r225).`},{tag:`e040`,title:`Trapped Chest`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`A locked treasure chest protected by a trap. May ignore it or attempt to open. Roll 1D6 — if result exceeds W&W the trap is sprung (r227). If survived: roll 1D6 for wealth code.`,note:`If result includes Pegasus winged mount (e188): instead it is a Pegasus talisman. Any magician/wizard/witch/priest/druid in party can use it to call an actual Pegasus (e188) as your mount (r204d).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Wealth Code (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`5`],[`2`,`25`],[`3`,`50`],[`4`,`60`],[`5`,`70`],[`6`,`100`]]}]},{tag:`e041`,title:`Vision Gem`,type:`magic`,image:!0,desc:`You find a large fixed stone with multiple glowing facets emanating ethereal light. Gazing into it you see visions of strange events and hidden secrets — past, present and future. Roll 1D6 for what you see.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Vision (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e176 – The Secret of the Ruins`],[`2`,`e144 – The Secret of Baron Huldra`],[`3`,`e145 – The Secret of Lady Aeravir`],[`4`,`e146 – The Secret of Count Drogat`],[`5`,`e147 – Clue to Treasure`],[`6`,`(Ψ) You are sat on a throne...`]]}]},{tag:`e042`,title:`Alcove of Sending`,type:`magic`,image:!1,desc:`A small alcove inscribed with runes. A magician/wizard/witch in party can decipher the runes and use the alcove to send voices and thoughts, commanding an audience with a distant lord. You are temporarily transported for the day and return before the evening meal. Followers given cannot return with you.`,note:`Can be used for: High Priest (e155), Town Mayor (e156), Village Elder (e177), Baron Huldra (e152), Lady Aeravir (e160), Count Drogat (e161). If discovered: Dwarf Lord (e059a), Halfling Mayor (e070a), Elf King (e166a), Wizard Lord (e068b), Hawk Lord (e117b), Giant Chieftain (e118b). Can only be used once per game — needs months to recharge.`},{tag:`e043`,title:`Small Altar`,type:`magic`,image:!0,desc:`A stone altar with an arcane symbol and an artefact resting on top. A magic spell shimmers around it. Wizard/magician/witch may identify the enchantment and roll 1D6 before deciding to approach. Others must decide before rolling. Roll 1D6 for artefact, then 1D6 for spell.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Artefact (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e186 – Magic Sword`],[`2`,`e189 – Charisma Talisman`],[`3`,`e191 – Resistance Ring`],[`4`,`e192 – Resurrection Necklace`],[`5`,`e193 – Shield of Light`],[`6`,`e194 – Royal Helm of the Northlands`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Spell (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`Magic shock field — no one can pass; auto 1 wound if tried`],[`3–4`,`Magic fire — roll 1D6 wounds to breach; may retreat or brave further exposure (roll 1D6 wounds again) to recover item`],[`5`,`Poison air — roll 1D6 poison wounds; item recovered in process`],[`6`,`Fear spell — harmless in reality. Roll 1D6: on 1 cannot approach; on 2–6 bravely recover item`]]}]},{tag:`e044`,title:`High Altar`,type:`magic`,image:!0,desc:`A high altar of godly power. A priest or monk in your party knows the invocations. May ask him to make the invocation and roll 1D6. Without such a follower the event ends.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Invocation Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`The monk or priest is engulfed in godly fires and dies`],[`2`,`Lightning destroys the altar — roll 1D6 wounds for each character from flying stones and fire`],[`3`,`Monk/priest takes 1 wound — wrong god's altar. No more invocations possible here`],[`4`,`(Ψ) Voice of the Gods gives an impossible riddle and a prophecy about your destiny. No more invocations possible`],[`5`,`Gods' voices promise a treasure — see e147`],[`6`,`A God appears pledging support. See usurpers struck down in a vision. Win game if you return to Northlands within 30 days. Endurance permanently -1. Must give all money to the Gods or be struck dead`]]}]},{tag:`e045`,title:`Arch of Travel`,type:`magic`,image:!0,desc:`A metal-banded archway inscribed with runes. A magician/wizard/witch can read the runes and use the arch to travel to any hex on the map. Without such a character, may plunge into the swirling mists and roll 1D6 for destination. Travel also moves time forward — roll 1D6 for extra days elapsed.`,note:`Arch travel is one-way only (from arch to any hex, not back to arch). Can be reused as long as a magician/wizard/witch is in your party.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Random Destination (1D6, without magic user)`,rows:[[`1`,`Hex 0408`],[`2`,`Hex 1210`],[`3`,`Hex 1909`],[`4`,`Hex 0323`],[`5`,`Hex 1119`],[`6`,`Hex 2018`]]}]},{tag:`e046`,title:`Gateway to Darkness`,type:`magic`,image:!1,desc:`One of the dreaded black portals of the Cult of Dark Wizards. Followers from south of the Tragoth River instantly recognise it. May ignore it or enter alone (all followers and mounts must be abandoned). Inside: a guardian creature (†S7, E7*) strikes first — must fight to the death, cannot escape.`,note:`If guardian killed: re-emerge into same hex but travel backwards in time — roll 2D6 and subtract from days elapsed. Followers lost to future. Gold and possessions still apply. Each time you re-enter: one additional guardian plus all previously killed ones return. Once entered, you know the secret and can use it without followers from south of Tragoth.`},{tag:`e047`,title:`Mirror of Reversal`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`An old mirror shows your self twisted into evil shape. The mirror-thing leaps out and attacks first (r220). It has your same Skill, Endurance, possessions and gold. Followers may help but each strike: roll 1D6 — on 5–6 the follower hits you by mistake.`,note:`If mirror-thing killed: +1 W&W (Ψ) from deeper self-understanding. Also doubles your possessions and wealth by gaining those of the mirror-thing.`},{tag:`e048`,title:`Fugitive`,type:`npc`,image:!1,desc:`Someone trying to avoid local justice. Will join as ally but deserts if you encounter Constables (e050) unless you fight and kill them, or whenever you enter any town, castle or temple. If Constables met in a town/castle: must escape immediately (r218). May alternatively fight the fugitive (r300).`,note:`Roll 1D6 for identity.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Fugitive Identity (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Swordsman adventurer (S7, E7, W1). On 6: swordswoman (S7, E6, W4) and become lovers (r228)`],[`2`,`Runaway slave (S2, E4, W0). On 6: slave woman and become lovers (r228). Local guide within 3 hexes`],[`3`,`Priest (S3, E3, W10). Gives half his wealth (r225). Heals 1 wound/day, +1 to Offering (r212)`],[`4`,`Magician (S3, E3, W5). Gives treasure clue — roll direction & distance, then e026. See e016 for magic powers`],[`5`,`Merchant (S2, E3, W15). Guide and bargain helper — half price for all purchases and bribes. Roll 1D6: on 5–6 has a horse`],[`6`,`Army deserter (S4, E4, W2). Roll 1D6: on 5–6 also has a horse`]]}]},{tag:`e049`,title:`Traveling Minstrel`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A bard (S2, E3, W4; female on 5–6). May ignore or invite to dinner (r215). If fed, he sings — roll 1D6 for each follower: on 4+ they will not desert regardless of today's events or food/lodging. After songs may start a conversation (r341).`,note:`If joined: may use desertion prevention once per day and you need not buy drinks at tavern (r230). Female bard: on 5+ becomes your lover (r228).`},{tag:`e050`,title:`Local Constabulary`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Only applies if within 3 hexes of a habitation on the map. If nearest is Ogon/Weshor: on 1–3 see e002. If nearest is Dwarf Mine: see e058. If Elf Haven: see e071. You encounter 1D6+1 officers (each S5, E4, W4). Roll 1D6: on 1–4 they are on foot (+2 to number unless in/nearest to village); on 5–6 they are mounted.`,note:`Add +2 to roll if you have visited nearest habitation and did not escape. No bonus if escaped (you are 'wanted'). Add +1 if you have never visited. If attacked: may surrender instead — move to nearest habitation and see e060. Mounts allow escape mounted (r312); flying party may use escape flying (r313).`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`surprised r308`,`attacked r306`,`surprised r308`],[`2`,`**attacked r306`,`hide r320`,`**attacked r307`],[`3`,`bribe-pass (10) r322`,`escape r311`,`**attacked r306`],[`4`,`bribe-pass (15) r323`,`escape r315`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`pass r329`,`hide r318`,`**attacked r304`],[`6`,`**attacked r306`,`attacked r305`,`surprise r303`],[`7`,`pass r325`,`pass r325`,`surprise r302`],[`8`,`pass r326`,`pass r325`,`surprise r301`]]}]},{tag:`e051`,title:`Bandits`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`You are ambushed by bandits who surprise you in combat (r220). The number of bandits exceeds your party size by two. One bandit is the leader (†S6, E6, W15); the rest are S5, E4, W1.`},{tag:`e052`,title:`Goblins`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`You sight a band of Goblins in the distance. Roll 2D6 for number (each S3, E3, W1) led by a Hobgoblin (†S6, E5, W5). You saw them first — may fight (r303), escape (r218) or follow (r219).`,note:`If following: after move (r219) roll 1D6 — if exceeds W&W, goblins discover you and attack (your party strikes first, r220). If not discovered: roll 1D6 for destination — 1→e054; 2–6→e053.`},{tag:`e053`,title:`Campsite`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`Roll 2D6 for exact campsite location. May elect to fight characters encountered or not. If not fighting: must hide (r218) in hex until tomorrow. If you fight and kill all: gain control of campsite.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Campsite Location (2D6)`,rows:[[`2`,`Small altar — if controlled see e043`],[`3`,`Previously unknown ruin — if controlled see e064`],[`4`,`Entrance to cave tombs — if controlled see e028`],[`5`,`Rendezvous with same-sized group — group now double size. Roll 1D6: if exceeds W&W they discover you and attack (you strike first); if not, roll 2D6 on this table again`],[`6–8`,`Open ground — no special further event`],[`9`,`Hidden by terrain — if controlled, roll again for exact location (re-roll 9s)`],[`10`,`Small building — if controlled may investigate. Roll 1D6: 1→e042; 2→e037; 3→e038; 4→e039; 5–6→Nothing`],[`11`,`Near place of magic — if controlled roll 1D6: 1→e032; 2→e036; 3→e043; 4→e044; 5→e045; 6→e046`],[`12`,`Near hidden magic — if controlled and have druid/priest/monk/magician/wizard/witch: roll 1D6: 1→e041; 2→e042; 3→e043; 4→e044; 5→e045; 6→e046`]]}]},{tag:`e054`,title:`Goblin Keep`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`A fortified tower, the Keep of a Goblin King, swarming with hundreds of goblins. A band spots you and charges. May attempt escape (r218) or fight. Roll 1D6 — if W&W exceeds roll you lose pursuing goblins. Otherwise surrender (e061) or fight.`,note:`If fighting: roll 3D6 goblins (each S3, E3, W1) + one hobgoblin leader (†S6, E5, W4). You strike first (r220). Cannot escape — fight to death or surrender (e061). Later visits: auto-captured unless roll less than W&W on 1D6 — if less, may raid for loot (r224b, guards are goblins S3, E3, W1). Adjacent hex end-of-day: roll 1D6, on 1–2 see e052 (+1 goblins).`},{tag:`e055`,title:`Orcs`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`You sight a band of Orcs. Roll 2D6 for number — one is orc chieftain (†S5, E6, W7), rest are warriors (S4, E5, W1). May fight (r303), escape (r218) or follow (r219). Roll 1D6: on 1–2 orcs have a captive — if killed and rescued, captive joins as ally (r334).`,note:`Captive identity (1D6, W0, 1 wound): 1→e003; 2→e006; 3→e049; 4→e069; 5→e172; 6→e073. If following: after move roll 1D6 — if exceeds W&W, orcs discover you (party strikes first, r220). If not: roll 1D6 — 1→e056; 2–6→e053. At campsite: may escape to adjacent hex (r218) or make surprise attack (r220).`},{tag:`e056`,title:`Orc Tower`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`The dark tower of an Orc Warlord, full of orcs and worse. An orc war-patrol led by a demi-Troll (†S8, E7, W10) spots you. Roll 1D6+1 for number of orcs in patrol (each S5, E5, W2). May surrender (e061), escape (r314) or attack (r306).`,note:`Later visits: auto-captured unless roll less than W&W on 1D6 — if less, may raid tower for loot (r224b, guards are orcs S5, E5, W2). Adjacent hex end-of-day: roll 1D6, on 1–2 see e055 (+1 orcs).`},{tag:`e057`,title:`Troll`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`A huge stone-skinned troll (†S8, E8, W15) confronts you with a terrifying roar. Roll 1D6 — if exceeds W&W the troll strikes first (r220), otherwise you strike first. A Troll automatically cures 1 wound at the end of each combat round.`,note:`If killed: troll skin worth 50 gold when buying food. Count Drogat prizes it as a gift (+1 audience roll). Counts as 1 load. If carrying skin when encountering another troll: that troll is S9 and strikes first!`},{tag:`e058`,title:`Band of Dwarves`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`A group of 1D6+1 dwarf warriors (each S5, E6, W10) with long beards and sharp axes. If your party is smaller: you spot them first and may follow (r219), hide (r218b), escape (r218a) or encounter normally. If equal or larger: they see you first and you must choose an encounter option.`,note:`If following: after move (r219) roll 1D6 — if equals or exceeds W&W, dwarves discover you and strike first (r220). If not: roll 1D6 — 1→e059; 2–6→e053. Mounts allow escape mounted (r312).`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`escape r311`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`bribe-join (30) r331`,`escape r314`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`bribe-hire (10) r332`,`escape r315`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`hirelings r339`,`hide r317`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`pass r328`,`hide r319`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`attacked r306`,`attacked r306`,`surprised r308`]]}]},{tag:`e059`,title:`Dwarf Mines`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`A great underground castle inhabited by Dwarves. Dwarf patrols intercept your party. Roll 1D6 (-1 if dwarf in party, +1 if elf) — if exceeds W&W you are all arrested (e060). Otherwise you may enter and undertake any castle actions (r203) including seeking audience with the Dwarf Lord (r211g). Normal food (r215) and lodging (r217) costs apply. Hired followers (r210) will be dwarves.`,note:`If dwarf in party: +1 to Seek News & Information (r209).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Audience with Dwarf Lord (1D6, +1 if giving gemstone worth 50+ gold)`,rows:[[`1`,`He is insulted — see e060 immediately`],[`2`,`Hears your story but remains stone-faced. May seek audience again`],[`3`,`Gives free food and lodging tonight as distinguished visitor. May seek audience again`],[`4`,`Gives you a purse of 50 gold. Cannot seek audience until next week`],[`5`,`Tells of rumours of lost dwarf mines — roll 1D6 for direction and distance. If you go there see e067. Cannot seek audience again`],[`6+`,`If dwarf in party: supports your cause, gives gems (e142) and a trusted dwarf guard (S6, E6, W10). Cannot seek audience again. If no dwarf: dismisses you until you have one`]]}]},{tag:`e060`,title:`Arrested`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You and your party are arrested by an overwhelming force and cannot resist. You are put into jail. Roll 1D6 for the gravity of the offence.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Offence Gravity (1D6)`,rows:[[`1 or less`,`e061 – Marked for Death`],[`2`,`e062 – Thrown in the Dungeon`],[`3–4`,`e063 – Imprisoned`],[`5–6`,`e060a – Minor Offence: held overnight with food & lodging provided. Fined 10 gold + 2 gold/character + 1 gold/mount. Unpaid characters/mounts are lost. If cannot pay for anyone (including yourself) see e063`]]}]},{tag:`e061`,title:`Marked for Death`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You have committed a very grave offence and the death penalty is demanded. You and your party are imprisoned with all money, possessions and mounts confiscated. Food and lodging provided while awaiting execution. Roll 1D6 at start of each day.`,note:`Escape: roll 1D6 for each other party member — 1–2 they escape with you; 3–6 unable or already executed. Use escape rules (r218) for destination. Escape takes the entire day.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Daily Prison Roll (1D6)`,rows:[[`6`,`You manage to escape — see Escape note`],[`1 (1–2 if captured by goblins/orcs/wizard/giant/Count Drogat)`,`You meet the headsman and are put to death`],[`Other`,`Continue to languish in prison`]]}]},{tag:`e062`,title:`Thrown in the Dungeon`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You are thrown into a deep dark dungeon. All other party members are imprisoned or sold as slaves and permanently lost (unless a lover, r228). All wealth, possessions and mounts lost. Food and lodging of a sort provided. Every full week in dungeon inflicts 1 poisoned wound due to unhealthy conditions.`,note:`Escape takes the entire day. After escape must find food for evening meal (r215).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Daily Dungeon Roll (2D6, +1 per full week imprisoned)`,rows:[[`2–9`,`Continue to languish in captivity`],[`10`,`See e062a – Fellow Prisoner joins as ally (r334) if you escape. Roll 1D6: 1→e003; 2→e006; 3→e049; 4→e069; 5→e172; 6→e073`],[`11+`,`You escape the dungeon (r218)`]]}]},{tag:`e063`,title:`Imprisoned`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You and your party are imprisoned. All money, possessions and mounts confiscated and lost. Food and lodging of sorts provided. Roll 1D6 at start of each day (+1 per full week imprisoned).`,note:`If you escape: roll 1D6 for each party member — on 5–6 they escape with you, otherwise lost. Use escape rules (r218). Escape takes the entire day, after which must find food for evening meal (r215). On roll of 1: see e062a.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Daily Prison Roll (1D6, +1 per full week)`,rows:[[`1`,`See e062a – Fellow Prisoner`],[`6`,`You escape — see note above`],[`Other`,`Remain captive`]]}]},{tag:`e064`,title:`Ruins`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You discover the hidden ruins of a crumbling old tower covered in twisting ivy creepers. May contain long-lost treasures or terrible dangers. You can undertake a search action (r208) on any following day you are in the hex, as if the ruins were marked on the map.`},{tag:`e065`,title:`Hidden Village`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`You discover a previously unknown small and peaceful village tucked away in the hills. May undertake any normal daily actions (r203) as if it were a village on the map, including food (r215) and lodging (r217). Cannot seek news and information (r209) here due to isolation. If a specific Habitation event is rolled: ignore the result, no event today.`},{tag:`e066`,title:`Secret Temple`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You find the secret temple of an obscure and feared cult. Guardian monks surround you. Roll 1D6 — if equals or exceeds W&W you and your party are arrested (e060, -1 to die roll). If less: you talk your way past and may enter. May undertake any daily actions allowed at a normal temple (r203). Normal food (r215) and lodging (r217) rules apply.`,note:`On later returns: guardians freely permit entrance with no new rolls needed. However if you kill anyone while in the temple or escape (r218) from it: next visit you will be immediately arrested (e060, -2 to die roll).`},{tag:`e067`,title:`Abandoned Mines`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`You come across apparently abandoned mines that look like they were once inhabited by dwarves. May avoid them or enter and investigate. If a dwarf is in your party: roll twice and choose either result.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Investigation Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e059 – Dwarf Mines (still inhabited!)`],[`2`,`e051 – Bandits`],[`3`,`e046 – Gateway to Darkness`],[`4`,`e045 – Arch of Travel`],[`5–6`,`e028 – Cave Tombs`]]}]},{tag:`e068`,title:`Wizard's Abode`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`You discover a wizard's home. Roll 1D6 to determine what you find.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Abode Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`e023 – Wizard (but with 1D6 henchmen and wealth 110 instead)`],[`3–4`,`e016`],[`5–6`,`e068a`]]}]},{tag:`e068a`,title:`Wizard Tower`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`A great tower looms before you. It is just like a castle for food (r215) and lodging (r217). However, the ruling wizard is jealous, and will tolerate no rivals. Any magicians, wizards or witches in your party are captured unless you elect to have your whole party escape (r218) from the hex. If you stay, on subsequent days you can study (r229) or seek an audience with the wizard (r211h). You cannot seek news (r209), hire followers (r210) or visit a tavern (r230) or market (r231) here. You may also attempt to rescue any followers captured by the wizard like a robbery (see r224b).`},{tag:`e068b`,title:`Audience with Wizard Lord`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You discover a wizard's home. Roll 1D6 to determine what you find.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Abode Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`0`,`He is angered by your interruptions – see e062`],[`1`,`He hears your story, but remains stone-faced, unwilling or unable to help.`],[`2`,`He listens to your story and offers you clues that might help your quest – see e147`],[`3`,`He hears your story and gives you a magical gift to help you – see e195.`],[`4`,`He listens with interest and shares with you legends of lost ages – see e176.`],[`5`,`He decides to help you in your quest but only if you help him. If you agree then he gives you his raven ‘familiar’ that adds +1 to your Wit & Wiles whilst with you. The wizard also enchants your sword – see e186. In return, when you receive any further magical objects (e183-e196), roll 1D6. On a 5-6, the wizard wants it for his collection and the raven vanishes with the object.`],[`6`,`The wizard listens to your story with growing anger. Upon learning the names of the northern usurpers he declares that they were the very ones who did him ill deeds many years ago. He immediately uses his powerful magic to transport you and his army to the Northland capital to retake your throne. You immediately win the game.`]]}]},{tag:`e069`,title:`Wounded Warrior`,type:`npc`,image:!1,desc:`You come across a heroic fighter near death. He was S7, E6, W0 but has four wounds. If you remain with him while he rests and heals, or bring him along while he heals, he will join your party as an ally at no cost except food (r215) and lodging (r217). Will not desert due to starvation (r216a).`},{tag:`e070`,title:`Halfling Village`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`A hidden village of halflings nestled among gentle rolling hills. May seek news (r209), seek audience (r211i), try for a job (r233), visit tavern (r230) or market (r231). Cannot hire followers (r210). Normal food (r215) and lodging (r217) rules apply. When seeking news (r209) or visiting tavern (r230) here: wined and dined so much that evening meal is free.`,note:`Killing a halfling (e008) in the village: arrested immediately (e060, -1 to roll). Killing any other character or escaping (r218) from village: halflings dislike you for the rest of the game — -1 to Seek News (r209) and Seek Audience (r211) rolls here.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Audience with Halfling Mayor (1D6, +1 if dwarf/halfling/wizard in party)`,rows:[[`1`,`He is insulted — see e060 immediately (+2 to roll)`],[`2`,`Hears story with interest, gives party free food tonight. Cannot see how to help. May seek audience again`],[`3`,`Gives free food and lodging tonight and as long as you stay. May seek audience again`],[`4`,`Gives entire party free food and lodging for stay, plus shares information (r209, +2 to roll). Cannot seek audience until next week`],[`5`,`Writes Letter of Recommendation (e157) to nearest town mayor and gives 40 gold. Cannot seek audience until letter is used. Party fed free tonight`],[`6+`,`If halfling or wizard in party: supports your cause — gives wealth 25 and his adventurous nephew joins (S3, E6, W7, guide within 2 hexes). Cannot seek audience again. Party fed and lodged free tonight. If no halfling/wizard: dismisses you until you have one`]]}]},{tag:`e071`,title:`Elf Band`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`A band of 1D6+1 elf warriors (each S5, E4, W7) in dark green cloaks armed with bows and long knives. All elves gain +1S in ranged combat (r220i). Subtract -1 W&W in this event. If elves join: +1 W&W while with you.`,note:`-1 to roll if elf in party, +1 if dwarf in party, +1 if event occurs in forest. If not in forest: may use escape mounted (r312) instead of rolling. Follow: make follow move (r219), roll 1D6 — 1→e166; 2–6→e053. May peacefully share campsite or fight elves for control (elves strike first, r220). May follow for several days before arriving at a castle.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`0`,`follow – see note`,`pass r325`,`surprise r301`],[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`escape-fly r313`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`inquiry r342`,`escape r315`,`attack r305`],[`3`,`conversation r341`,`hide r318`,`attacked r306`],[`4`,`pass r325`,`hide r320`,`attacked r307`],[`5`,`follow – see note`,`attacked r306`,`surprised r308`],[`6`,`surprised r309`,`surprised r309`,`surprised r310`],[`7+`,`surprised r309`,`escape-fly r313`,`attack r305`]]}]},{tag:`e072`,title:`Contest`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`The Lord is holding a contest in the courtyard. Entry costs 5 gold. Must fight and win 3 battles alone (no party help but may use possessions) to meet the arena Champion. Roll 1D6 for each opponent (r305). Win 1st round: 10 gold; 2nd: 50 gold; 3rd: 100 gold.`,note:`If lose 2nd or 3rd round: out of competition but healers restore full health at cost of all previous winnings. If survive 3rd round: healers restore full health then fight Champion (S8, E8) — win: 200 gold and immediate audience with local Lord. Knocking Mercenary/Amazon/Champion unconscious and sparing them: only half gold but on 4–6 they become a loyal ally (r334).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Opponent (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Wolf — S3, E3`],[`2`,`Wild Boar — S5 (S8 on first round due to charge), E4`],[`3`,`Hunting Cat — S6, E3`],[`4–5`,`Mercenary* — S5, E4`],[`6`,`Amazon* — S6, E5`]]}]},{tag:`e073`,title:`Witch`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a witch. Roll 1D6: on 1–4 an old crone (†S1, E3, W12); on 5–6 a young witch (†S2, E3, W5). Roll 1D6 — if exceeds W&W she is hostile; if equal she ignores you; if less she is friendly.`,note:`Hostile: party must escape (r218) immediately. Roll 1D6 for each character — on 6 they are turned into a frog and lost. If you are turned into a frog: a lover/magician/wizard/friendly witch can turn you back, otherwise you lose the game. If joined: may use transfiguration on opponents with S7 or less instead of normal strike (as long as not attacked). On 5+: has a broomstick for airborne travel (r204d). In forest hex: may forage food (r215b) for herself. Young witch: on 6 you become lovers (r228).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Friendly Witch Action (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`Joins your party as ally (r334)`],[`3–4`,`Can be hired to join your party (r333)`],[`5–6`,`Gives you a gift (e195) then vanishes`]]}]},{tag:`e074`,title:`Giant Spiders`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Your entire party is trapped by the sticky webs of 1D6 giant spiders (each S4, E4). Each character's Skill is reduced by -1. Spiders scuttle towards you to attack — very likely surprise (r309). Spiders inflict only poisoned wounds. A character not attacking in a round can cut the webs to restore their Skill.`,note:`If spiders killed: venom of 3 spiders can make one vial of a poison drug (e185).`},{tag:`e075`,title:`Wolves`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Postponed until after evening meal (r215). At night a pack of wolves attacks. Roll 2D6+1 for number (each S3, E3). May surprise you (r309). Cannot escape — but roll 1D6 at start of combat: +1 per follower, +1 per food unit thrown to wolves, +2 per mount sacrificed. If total is less than number of wolves they attack; otherwise they leave.`,note:`At end of each combat round: if wolves still alive, they kill one of your mounts (only mount lost, not loads). Wolf pelts (1 load each) sell at village/town/castle for 3 gold each — roll 1D6 per wolf killed, on 4–6 pelt is good enough to sell.`},{tag:`e076`,title:`Great Hunting Cat`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`A great hunting cat (S6, E3) ambushes and surprises you in combat (r220), selecting one victim (r343). As soon as the cat kills its victim it disappears into the wilds, carrying off the victim and any wealth or possessions they carried. Victim's mount (if any) is also killed but left behind. Victim cannot escape (r218) but rest of party can.`,note:`May skin the great cat — pelt (1 load) sells at any town or castle for 8 gold. Roll 1D6: on 4–6 pelt is good enough to sell.`},{tag:`e077`,title:`Herd of Wild Horses`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You surprise a herd of wild and magnificent horses. Each character in your party can capture one, giving you that many additional mounts. Must spend tomorrow resting (r203) to train the mounts. A druid can cast a spell to train them instantly with no rest needed. A magician/wizard/witch may also know the taming spell on 4–6 (1D6).`,note:`May sell any horses at any village/town/castle for 6 gold pieces each.`},{tag:`e078`,title:`Bad Going`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Ignore this event unless travelling (r204) on non-winged mounts such as horses, and not along a road or entering a habitation. The terrain is proving difficult. Must either halt for the day to explore alternate routes, or risk injuries.`,note:`If continuing normal travel: roll 1D6-3. If result is 1 or more, that many horses have broken legs or thrown shoes and are lost as mounts after the move — may need to reorganise loads (r206) or cache items (r214). If this event occurs after your last travel move: difficult terrain applies to any travel (r204) the following day. If a non-travel action is selected tomorrow: you are presumed to explore alternate routes in the course of that action, ending this event.`},{tag:`e079`,title:`Heavy Rains`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Cold driving rains hinder man and beast. Must stop moving today. Roll 1D6 for each character in party — on 5–6 the character catches cold and suffers 1 wound.`,note:`Next morning: roll 1D6 — on 4–6 rains continue. If using mounts they may catch pneumonia — roll 1D6 each, on 5–6 they fall sick and die. May lead animals at walking speed without risk. If travelling at all (r204): any characters not yet ill must risk it. Day after tomorrow: weather clears unless this event occurs again.`},{tag:`e080`,title:`Pixies`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`A group of small flying sprites appear and dance around your party. Unless you have a druid/magician/wizard/witch/elf/halfling in your party, they dance away and the encounter ends. If you do have such a character: the pixies may stop to grant a boon — roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Pixie Boon (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Nothing of use`],[`2`,`Advice leading to treasure — roll 1D6 for direction and distance in hexes, then see e026`],[`3`,`Lead you to an ancient cache (e038)`],[`4–5`,`Provide a magical gift (e195)`],[`6`,`Give you a winged pegasus mount (e188)`]]}]},{tag:`e081`,title:`Mounted Patrol`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You encounter a mounted patrol of soldiers. Roll 1D6 for number in patrol (each S6, E5, W4). One is the leader with W10 instead.`,note:`If party has winged mounts or ability to fly: may use flying escape (r313) instead of rolling for evade option.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`escape mounted r312`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`pass r327`,`escape mounted r312`,`attack r305`],[`3`,`bribe-pass (10) r322`,`escape r314`,`attack r305`],[`4`,`bribe-pass (8) r324`,`hide r318`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`attacked r306`,`attacked r306`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`surprised r308`,`surprised r308`,`surprised r308`]]}]},{tag:`e082`,title:`Spectre`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`An unearthly spectre from the astral plane appears in the midst of your party casting a hideous miasma. Roll 1D6 — on 6 see e200. Otherwise one character is the spectre's victim (r343). Druids/magicians/wizards/witches/priests/monks must be first choices. The victim is turned to smoke and taken to the astral plane, never to be seen again.`,note:`If you are the victim: you are lost and the game ends. Since a spectre is a magical being it can be stopped using any possession that protects against magic attacks or injury.`},{tag:`e083`,title:`Wild Boar Charges`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`A huge wild boar charges toward your party, surprising you in combat (r220). The boar normally has S5, E4 but its surprise strike is made with S8 due to its fearsome charge. Determine which character in your party is the target (r343). The boar continues selecting victims until you escape or kill it.`,note:`If boar killed: roll 2D6 for number of food units it provides.`},{tag:`e084`,title:`Bear Comes to Dinner`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`You are about to sit down for a meal when a large ferocious black bear wanders into your campsite. The bear randomly attacks one character (r343) after another until all are killed, the bear is killed, or you escape. Bear strikes first (r220) and has S6, E5.`,note:`If bear killed: pelt sells at town/castle for 10 gold (counts as 2 loads). Roll 1D6 — on 4–6 pelt is good enough to sell. Alternatively may wear pelt as a cloak — keeps you warm negating 1 wound from cold effects of e086. Cloak may also impress Baron Huldra in an audience.`},{tag:`e085`,title:`Narrow Ledges`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Your party passes along narrow ledges overhanging deep gorges. Roll 2D6 for each character on foot — on 12 they slip and fall to their death. Roll 1D6 for each non-winged mount — on 6 the mount stumbles and falls to its death carrying any rider and loads. If entered hex mounted: entire party considered on mounts.`,note:`If you personally slip or your mount falls: you are presumed to catch a ledge somewhere down the cliff and survive, but roll 1D6+1 for wounds suffered. Then roll 1D6 to see if party finds you — on 5–6 they do; any other result means the rest of your party disappears with all mounts, possessions and wealth they were carrying.`},{tag:`e086`,title:`High Pass`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`To leave the hex by any direction except the one you entered, you must travel through a high alpine pass covered in deep snow. On your next travel action (r204), unless leaving by the hex you entered, you must take the high pass as long as you aren't lost. Roll 2D6 for the effects.`,note:`Due to the cold: cannot cache wealth or possessions here — they must be abandoned in the snows.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`High Pass Effects (2D6)`,rows:[[`7 or less`,`No effect and travel is not hindered`],[`8`,`Each character in party suffers 1 wound due to cold`],[`9`,`Each character suffers 2 wounds, mounts die, no caches*`],[`10`,`Each character suffers 3 wounds, mounts die, no caches*`],[`11+`,`Each character suffers 4 wounds, mounts die, no caches*`]]}]},{tag:`e087`,title:`Impassable Woods`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`The forest becomes thicker and thicker with choking brambles and shadows until further travel is almost impossible. Roll 1D6 — on 1 see e198b. Unless you have an elf or druid in your party: can only leave this hex by travelling back to the hex you came from. Lost rolls (r205) from this hex have a +1 penalty.`,note:`If entered from another direction this hex could be passable — only this route is blocked.`},{tag:`e088`,title:`Rock Fall`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`Travelling along a steep-sided gorge, a rock fall begins above you. Roll 1D6 for each character; mounts without riders are rolled for separately.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Rock Fall Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–4`,`Rocks miss — no effect`],[`5`,`Character suffers 1 wound from flying rock chips. A riderless mount is caught by a rolling boulder, breaks a leg and must be killed`],[`6`,`Character is hit by heavy rock — roll 1D6+1 for wounds suffered. A riderless mount is hit and killed instantly with all load buried beneath rocks and also lost`]]}]},{tag:`e089`,title:`Impassable Morass`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The swamp turns into an impassable morass of weeds, quicksand, water and muck. Roll 1D6 — on 1 see e090. Unless you have a druid or witch in your party who can navigate your way: can only leave this hex by moving to the hex you came from. Lost rolls (r205) from this hex have a +1 penalty.`,note:`The hex might be passable if entered from another direction — only this route is blocked.`},{tag:`e090`,title:`Quicksand`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`Your party stumbles into quicksand. Roll 1D6 for each character or mount. If a character is mounted: may either roll for both together as a character, or sacrifice the mount and its load to jump to safety.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Quicksand Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`Character or mount struggles out of the quicksand without harm`],[`4`,`Character or mount escapes only if another character or mount is already free to pull it out — otherwise it sinks and dies`],[`5`,`Character struggles out using a vine — mount lost with everything carried`],[`6`,`Character or mount is trapped in the deepest part of the swamp — cannot win free and automatically lost with everything carried`]]}]},{tag:`e091`,title:`Poison Snake`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Your party unknowingly walks over the lair of a poison snake. One character (r343) suffers snake-bite poison wounds — roll 1D6 for the number of poison wounds received.`,note:`If a witch is in your party: she might gain some poison from it — roll 1D6, on 5–6 see e185.`},{tag:`e092`,title:`Flood`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Rains swell the water level of the marsh causing flooding. Your party is stranded on a small knoll and unable to travel further today. Roll 1D6 at the start of tomorrow — on 5–6 waters subside and you can travel again (r204). Otherwise remain stranded and cannot make any travel (r204), escape (r218), hunt (r215) or follow (r219) actions.`,note:`Roll 1D6 at the start of each day until travel is possible once more.`},{tag:`e093`,title:`Poison Plants`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You notice poison plants around you today, often a sign of greater evil. Cannot hunt for food (r215) today. Roll 1D6 for an additional event.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Additional Event (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e034 – Spectre of the Inner Tomb`],[`2`,`e032 – Ghosts`],[`3`,`e033 – Warrior Wraiths`],[`4`,`e074 – Giant Spiders`],[`5–6`,`No additional event`]]}]},{tag:`e094`,title:`Crocodiles`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Very large and very hungry crocodiles suddenly attack your party. Roll 1D6 for the number of crocodiles (each S4, E6). If in a swamp: cannot escape from them (r220e) in battle. Crocodiles always strike first in combat (r220) and in a swamp will also achieve surprise (r220d).`},{tag:`e095`,title:`Mounts at Risk`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The strain of travelling is exhausting your mounts. Roll 1D6 for each mount in your party.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Mount Condition (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–4`,`Mount still in fair condition — no special effect`],[`5`,`Mount failing — unless allowed to rest (r203) one day, it will die after tomorrow`],[`6`,`Mount in serious condition — cannot continue further and must be killed`]]}]},{tag:`e096`,title:`Mounts Die`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The adverse conditions finally take their toll on your mounts. All mounts including winged ones must roll 1D6 at the end of each day until they die or recover.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Daily Mount Roll (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Mount recovering — do not roll any further for it`],[`2`,`Mount recovering if it did not travel today (do not roll further). If the mount did travel today: still failing, roll again at end of tomorrow`],[`3`,`Mount still failing but not dead yet — roll again at end of tomorrow`],[`4–6`,`Mount cannot take any more and dies now`]]}]},{tag:`e097`,title:`Marsh Gas and Rot`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Your entire party has unwittingly travelled into an area full of flesh-rot disease and mind-destroying marsh gas. Roll 1D6 for each character (yourself first).`,note:`If a witch is in your party: she may collect the marsh gas to make a death mist potion (e190).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Marsh Gas Effect (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–4`,`Character dies a mindless and raving idiot while his flesh rots from his bones before your eyes`],[`5–6`,`Character escapes unharmed`]]}]},{tag:`e098`,title:`Dragon`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter an enormous winged fire-breathing dragon (†S12, E12). Roll 1D6: on 1–2 you have found the dragon in its lair [Site] with wealth 110 and wealth 60; on 3–6 it has only wealth 30. Add +2 to encounter roll if in the dragon's lair.`,note:`In combat (r220): cannot escape (r218). Before combat starts: roll 1D6 for each other party member — on 1 they flee in fear and desert you (unless a lover, r228). If dragon killed: a dragon's eye is valued by high priests and may help gain an audience. Worth 20 gold each, counts as 1 load (r206), can be sold to any merchant or in any town/castle/temple. Asleep: may attack (r303), escape (r218) or try to steal treasure — roll 1D6: if exceeds W&W dragon awakes (r306); if equal cannot get any but may try again; if less than W&W steal Wealth 25. On talk roll of 1: if dragon amused by your charm it gives you a clue (e147).`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6, +2 in lair)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`**pass r329`,`escape flying r313`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`**pass r329`,`escape r315`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`attacked r307`,`hide r318`,`attack r305`],[`4`,`attacked r307`,`hide r320`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`surprised r308`,`attacked r306`,`surprised r308`],[`6`,`surprised r309`,`surprised r308`,`surprised r309`],[`7+`,`asleep*`,`asleep*`,`asleep*`]]}]},{tag:`e099`,title:`Roc`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a roc (†S10, E8), a gigantic bird that swoops down on your party. May choose to evade, fight or attempt to tame it. To tame: sacrifice 4 food units or a mount as bait, then roll 1D6 and add W&W (+2 if wizard/magician/druid/witch in party).`,note:`Taming result: 2–8 roc attacks (r306); 9–10 roc grabs food and flies away; 11+ roc is tamed and acts as a mount for up to 2 characters but will not enter any habitation hex. May forage for food at night and does not require normal food. If roc killed: may cut off its beak as a trophy (1 load, r206). Count Drogat especially prizes it and may help gain an audience. Beak worth 35 gold and can be sold to any merchant or in any town/castle/temple.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`escape flying r313`,`surprise r301`],[`2`,`escape flying r313`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`hide r317`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`hide r319`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`attack r305`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`surprised r308`,`surprised r308`]]}]},{tag:`e100`,title:`Griffon`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`You meet a winged griffon (†S8, E6, W12). The griffon is intelligent and could serve as both a party member and a winged mount. However griffons are normally quite independent and ferocious and this one looks especially unfriendly.`,note:`After choosing your option: roll 1D6 — on 6 the griffon has a mate nearby (identical stats) who joins for your encounter. If joined: adds +1 to hunting score and can feed itself without using your food stores (r215). Will leave if you enter any habitation hex. If griffon killed: may remove its claws as a trophy (1 load). Especially valued by Lady Aeravir of Aeravir Castle and may help gain an audience. Claws worth 25 gold and can be sold to any merchant or in any town/castle/temple.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk**`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`escape-fly r313`,`surprise r303`],[`2`,`plead comrades r337`,`escape r315`,`attack r305`],[`3`,`attacked r306`,`hide r317`,`attack r305`],[`4`,`attacked r307`,`hide r318`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`surprised r308`,`pass r325`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`surprised r309`,`attacked r306`,`surprised r308`]]}]},{tag:`e101`,title:`Harpies`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter harpies, bird-women each with S5 (S6 when defending against an attack), E4, W4. Roll 1D6 for number encountered: 1–3→1; 4–5→2; 6→3.`,note:`Cannot choose Talk option if you have a priest/monk/elf/halfling in your party unless dismissed. If harpies join: their wings allow airborne travel without a winged mount and they travel like a normal mount. If entering a habitation: roll 1D6 before evening meal — on 1–4 see e017 and cannot stay unless you dismiss the harpies.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`escape-fly r313`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`pass r329`,`pass r325`,`attack r304`],[`3`,`looter r340`,`pass r326`,`attack r305`],[`4`,`looter r340`,`pass r327`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`surprised r308`,`pass r328`,`attack r306`],[`6`,`surprised r310`,`pass r329`,`surprised r309`]]}]},{tag:`e102`,title:`Light Rainstorm`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You must land immediately and cannot travel further today due to heavy cloud cover and bad weather. Roll 1D6 for each character in your party — on 6 the character catches cold and suffers 1 wound.`},{tag:`e103`,title:`Bad Headwinds`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You cannot move faster than two hexes flying today. If this event occurs after you have travelled to the third hex today: you must land in the last hex (second hex entered) instead and end your travel there.`},{tag:`e104`,title:`Good Tailwinds`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`There is a gentle breeze blowing and the skies ahead are clear and bright. You can fly one extra hex today if you wish. Normal travel rules (r204) apply when moving.`},{tag:`e105`,title:`Storm Clouds Ahead`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You can land immediately in the hex you just entered and avoid any risk. Alternatively if you continue flying today: before checking for lost or a travel event, roll 1D6 for what happens.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Continue Flying Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e103 – Bad Headwinds`],[`2`,`e102 – Light Rainstorm`],[`3`,`e079 – Heavy Rains`],[`4–5`,`No effect`],[`6`,`Party blown out of control by gigantic winds — roll 1D6 for random direction then 1D3 for hexes. You crash into the final hex: flying mount is killed, roll 1D6 for wounds suffered. All other party members are blown off course and lost to you`]]}]},{tag:`e106`,title:`Heavy Overcast`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`Thick black clouds obscure your vision and you realise you are becoming lost. Roll 1D6 for a random direction and move one hex in that direction. Roll 1D6 for each additional character or un-ridden mount in your party — on 6 that character/mount disappears into the overcast and is lost. The overcast skies force you and the remainder of your party to land in the new hex — no further travel today.`},{tag:`e107`,title:`Falcon Scout`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`A friendly falcon joins your party if you offer it one unit of food (r215) right now. For the rest of today you cannot get lost. Tonight at the evening meal (r215): if you offer a second food unit, roll 1D6 — on 1–5 the falcon (S1, E1) will remain throughout tomorrow as a guide also.`,note:`This can continue day after day provided you feed it a food unit at the evening meal and roll anything but a 6. Normal desertion rules for guides (r205a) do not apply to the falcon.`},{tag:`e108`,title:`Hawk-men Attack`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`A group of hawk-men swoop down on your party beating their wings. Roll 1D6+1 for the number of hawk-men (each S7, E5, W7). They surprise your party in combat (r220). Cannot escape from the combat — must fight to the death or surrender.`,note:`If you surrender: they capture you and take you to their castle amongst the clouds (e117a) at an adjacent hex — roll 1D6 for the random direction travelled. You are then thrown into a dungeon (e062).`},{tag:`e109`,title:`Wild Pegasus`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You encounter a wild pegasus. Each character in your party is allowed one attempt to capture it — roll 1D6. On 5+ (4+ if attempting character is a wizard/magician/druid/elf/witch) the character captures the pegasus and may add it as a winged mount to your party.`,note:`A pegasus acts like a normal mount for fodder and stabling (r217) and will always be stolen by thieves if not stabled.`},{tag:`e110`,title:`Air Spirit`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter an air spirit but will only recognise it if a magician/wizard/witch/druid/priest/monk is in your party. If you cannot recognise it: event ends. If recognised: may attempt communication by rolling 1D6.`,note:`If roll equals or exceeds W&W: communication fails — the air spirit becomes confused and blows you off course. Roll 1D6 for random direction then 1D6 for number of hexes blown in that direction. Move party to new hex and continue with daily actions (r203). If roll is less than W&W: successfully talk with the air spirit — roll 2D6 and move up to that many hexes immediately without risk of getting lost and with no new travel events except for the last hex (where a normal travel event must be determined).`},{tag:`e111`,title:`Storm Demon`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a powerful Demon of storms that attacks your party. If you have any magicians/wizards/witches/druids/priests/monks in your party: each can attempt to stop the demon by rolling 1D6 — on 6 the demon attack is blocked; any other result means their magic has no effect.`,note:`If unable to block the attack: your entire party is blown away to be lost or killed. You crash in a random adjacent hex where your winged mount is killed and you roll 1D6 for wounds suffered. Your wealth and possessions you carried are intact but the rest of your party is lost.`},{tag:`e112`,title:`Meet Eagle Clan`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`While airborne you encounter 1D6 eagles in flight (each S4, E3, W1). If your party has a character or mount other than humans/elves/halflings/pegasus mounts: must select evade or fight option. Wizards/witches/magicians/druids/priests/monks are presumed human unless specified otherwise.`,note:`If following: must abandon all party members without winged mounts or ability to fly. If eagles join: need not pay them gold each day — eagles can feed themselves each day but will leave if you end a day in any habitation, rejoining you when back in the wilds.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Evade`,`Follow*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`pass r325`,`ambush e113`,`attack r304`],[`2`,`pass r325`,`hunt e114`,`attacked r305`],[`3`,`pass r326`,`hunt e114`,`attacked r306`],[`4`,`pass r326`,`eyrie e115`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`**conversation r341`,`eyrie e115`,`surprised r308`],[`6`,`**inquiry r342`,`allies e116`,`surprised r309`]]}]},{tag:`e113`,title:`Eagle Ambush`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`The eagles lead you to a remote craggy area when more eagles suddenly appear. Roll 1D6 for the number of additional eagles that arrive (each S4, E3, W1). All of these make a surprise attack on your party (r220d). Any characters who cannot fly (by magical means or through winged mounts) may not escape the combat.`},{tag:`e114`,title:`Eagle Hunt`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Eagles are hunting and they join your party for the hunt. This ends all travel today. For the evening meal (r215) you can hunt regardless of hex type — add +2 to your normal Skill when doing so. You and the eagles will camp on a high crag and do not count as landing in this hex for the day.`,note:`Tomorrow the eagles leave ending the event. You can either land in the hex (see travel table for possible event r207) before pursuing any action you wish (r203), or you can leave the hex by travel (r204). If you leave the hex: advice from the eagles acts as if you had a guide for tomorrow only.`},{tag:`e115`,title:`Eagle Eyrie`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`The eagles lead you to their eyrie where you meet their flock and make friends. Tonight you receive free food (r215) for your entire party. Do not roll for landing in the hex as the eyrie replaces the normal landing event. Later in the game you can return to the eagle eyrie for free food but only if you make airborne travel into the eyrie hex and do not make a normal landing.`,note:`The first time you leave the eyrie: roll 1D6 — on 4–6 one of the eagles from the clan joins your party. The eagle (S4, E4, W0) will act as a guide (r205a) and will not desert if you still get lost. It can feed itself each day on the wing but will leave if you end a day in any habitation then rejoin you when back in the wilds. The eagle can also act as a winged mount for a halfling.`},{tag:`e116`,title:`Eagle Allies`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The eagles lead you to their high council and after hearing your tale decide to support your quest for the Northlands kingdom. After the council your entire party is feasted and your evening meals (r215) provided by the eagles. They then give you a warrior band of eagles to join your party — roll 2D6 for the number (each S4, E3, W0).`,note:`The eagles may act as guides if you wish, will not desert (r205a) and are able to feed each day on the wing without using your food, but will leave if you end a day in a habitation.`},{tag:`e117`,title:`Castle in the Clouds`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You see an enormous and incredible castle floating in the clouds. You may fly past, ending the event, or if you decide to investigate, roll 1D6:`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Roll (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`e118a – Cloud Giant's Castle`],[`3`,`The castle is deserted — may search it as a ruin (r208)`],[`4`,`Everyone in the castle is dead from a plague — see e133`],[`5–6`,`e117a – Hawk-man Castle`]]}]},{tag:`e117a`,title:`Hawk-man Castle`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`[Site] Unless brought here (e108), roll 1D6; if the roll exceeds your Wit & Wiles you are captured (e062) by Hawk-men guards. Otherwise, you are allowed to enter their magnificent castle. Due to the remote nature of the castle, you cannot attempt actions normally allowed at a castle (r203) but can seek an audience with the Hawk Lord who rules there (r211j). Like any castle, you must spend the normal amounts for food (r215) and lodging (r217). Anyone you meet in the castle will be Hawk-men.`},{tag:`e117b`,title:`Audience with Hawk Lord`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`You are brought before the mighty Lord of the castle. Roll 1D6:`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Roll (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`He is insulted and orders you thrown in a cell - see e063 immediately.`],[`2`,`He hears your story, but is unconcerned by the problems in the north. However, you are free to seek audiences with him again.`],[`3`,`He hears your story and holds a feast for your party, giving you free food and lodging tonight, as his guests. You are free to seek audiences with him again.`],[`4`,`The Hawk Lord holds a feast for you tonight as his guests so you have free food and lodging for your party. During the feast, the King discusses with you some information you may find useful – see e147. You may not seek an audience again.`],[`5`,`The Hawk Lord agrees to help your campaign. You are feasted and lodged tonight, and tomorrow given a bodyguard of 1D6 Hawk-men each S7, E5, W4 who join your party at no cost but will leave if you ever enter a habitation. You may not seek an audience again.`],[`6+`,`On hearing you are alive he reveals that he was an old friend of your father. The Hawk Lord immediately forms an advance war party and will lead it himself! The Lord is S8, E8, W60, and has with him 6 Hawk-men, each S7, E5, W4. You are fed and lodged tonight at a huge feast, then fly north starting tomorrow. If you return to a hex north of the Tragoth River you win the game.`]]}]},{tag:`e118`,title:`Giant`,type:`npc`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a 12-foot tall giant (†S9, E8, W10), with a large, bushy beard and a very big club. Giants are normally hostile but this one could be reasonable? Your options are:`,note:`The giant is too big to ride a mount, so you can't use mounted travel (r204) while he is with your party. He also eats double the normal amount of food (r215) each day and must have his own room if lodging at a habitation (r217). Roll 1D6 - on a ‘6’, he reveals the location of a castle (e118a) in the closest mountain hex.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:``,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`inquiry r342`,`escape flying r313`,`surprise r301`],[`2`,`plead comrades r337`,`escape mounted r312`,`surprise r302`],[`3`,`looter r340`,`hide r316`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`pass r328`,`hide r317`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`attacked r306`,`hide r319`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`surprised r308`,`hide r320`,`attacked r306`]]}]},{tag:`e118a`,title:`Giant’s Castle`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`[Site] You discover an enormous castle inhabited by giants. Roll 1D6 (with -1 to roll if you have a giant in your party) and unless the result is less than your Wit & Wiles, you are captured (e062) by giant guards. Otherwise, you are allowed to enter... Due to the remote nature of the castle, you cannot attempt actions normally allowed (r203) but can seek an audience with the Giant Chieftain who rules there (r211k) or rob him (r224b). In this castle you need only spend half the normal amounts for food (r215) and lodging (r217) as the castle (and food served) is so big!`},{tag:`e118b`,title:`Audience with Giant Chieftain`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You have an audience with the bad-tempered Giant Chieftain of the castle. Roll 1D6 for the result, and add +1 if you have a giant in your party:`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Roll (1D6)`,rows:[[`1-2`,`He roars with glee at your plight, see e062 immediately.`],[`3`,`He hears your story, but remains stone-faced, unwilling or unable to help. However, you are free to seek audiences with him again.`],[`4`,`He hears your pleas with some favour, and gives you a purse of 50 gold. You cannot seek another audience with him.`],[`5`,`He hears your story with interest and tells you of rumours of a lost giant treasure – see e147. You cannot seek another audience with him.`],[`6+`,`If your party includes a giant, he will support your cause. If not, he dismisses you and refuses any further audiences. If he supports you, he will give you a magical treasure (see e195) and one of his trusted giants (S9, E8, W10) to join your party. You cannot seek another audience with him.`]]}]},{tag:`e119`,title:`Flash Flood`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Your party is travelling down a valley when a roaring wall of water rushes toward you. Roll 1D6 for each member of your party — on 5–6 the character cannot scramble up the bank fast enough and is carried away. This includes the character, any mount and any wealth or possessions. Those carried away are permanently lost.`,note:`If you are carried away: any mount of yours is killed, roll 1D6 for wounds suffered, and you can only rescue one load of material from what you were carrying (r206). All the rest is lost and you are so far separated from your party that they give you up for dead.`},{tag:`e120`,title:`Exhaustion`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The burning days and freezing nights sap the strength of your party. Mounts cannot be ridden and the load capacity of all men and mounts (r206) is halved. Each character in your party suffers 1 wound. No rest or healing of wounds (r222) is allowed until you leave the desert or are in an oasis hex.`,note:`Mounts recover normal abilities whenever you leave the desert and rest one day, or rest a day at an oasis.`},{tag:`e121`,title:`Sunstroke`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The heat of the sun is unexpected. Roll 1D6 for each character and mount in your party — on 6 they collapse from sunstroke. Mounts that collapse must be left to die. Characters that collapse must be carried even if that means other loads must be abandoned (r206).`,note:`If you suffer sunstroke and are not carried for the rest of the day: suffer 1D6 wounds but revive in time for evening meal (r215) — however unable to hunt. If your party continues carrying sunstroke victims: no special effect except sunstroke victims cannot participate in hunting. All characters recover from sunstroke at the evening meal. If you suffer sunstroke and no one can carry you: other characters leave you behind and disappear.`},{tag:`e122`,title:`Raftsmen`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You meet raftsmen at the side of the river. For 1 gold piece they will transport you across along with any members of your party and mounts that you wish. Alternatively starting tomorrow you can hire them for travel up or down the river (r213). Cost is 2 gold per raft (each raft can carry 40 loads).`,note:`If you are not next to a river: there is no event.`},{tag:`e123`,title:`Knight at the Bridge`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`You find a small bridge to cross the river. However, a knight in armour (S8, E8, W30) stands at the opposite end, challenging any who wish to cross. You can only cross if you personally engage him in combat (r220). You may refuse the combat and end travel for the day, but roll 1D6 for each character with you, a 6 indicates he deserts due to your cowardice. If you engage in combat, roll 1D6 at the start of each round, you strike first if the roll is 4+, otherwise the knight strikes first. If either you or the knight is knocked unconscious, the duel ends.`,note:`You may give up the combat and end travel for the day after any round without needing to escape (r220e). If you are knocked unconscious, crossing is prohibited, and travel ends for the day. If the knight is knocked unconscious you can leave him here and continue to travel, or you can halt for the day, treat his wounds, and allow him to join you. No wages need be paid, but food (r215) and lodging (r217) must be supplied.`},{tag:`e124`,title:`Raft`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You cross the river using a quickly built raft. Roll 1D6 to determine if an unfortunate event occurs during the crossing.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Crossing Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`Nothing happens — may use the raft tomorrow (r213)`],[`3`,`e094 – Crocodiles`],[`4`,`e125 – Raft Overturns`],[`5`,`e126 – Raft caught in Current`],[`6`,`e127 – Raft in Rough Water`]]}]},{tag:`e125`,title:`Raft Overturns`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The raft is caught in an eddy, hits a rock and overturns. Everyone in your party including mounts swims to shore. However all wealth and all possessions not carried by characters are lost.`},{tag:`e126`,title:`Raft caught in Current`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Your raft is swept downriver — move one hex down-river (r213). Then roll 1D6 for each mount and character in your party — on 6 they are lost overboard and drown.`,note:`If you fall overboard: you can swim to shore losing all your wealth, possessions and mount, while your party disappears down river on the raft and is lost.`},{tag:`e127`,title:`Raft in Rough Water`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Your raft hits white water rapids — all food stores piled on it are lost overboard. All characters, mounts, wealth and possessions are saved. The raft continues its journey and you land or continue without further incident.`},{tag:`e128`,title:`Merchant`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You meet a friendly merchant. You can either pass him, ending this encounter, or you can stop to chat and barter. If you stop, roll 2D6 and consult below:`,note:`You may also attack the merchant, striking first in combat (r220). The merchant (S2, E3, W60) has 1D6-2 henchmen (each S5, E4, W4). If in a habitation, at the end of a combat round roll 1D6 - on 5+, 1D6+1 local guards arrive to defend the merchant (each S4, E4, W4). You may surrender to the guards (see e063).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Trade Result (2D6)`,rows:[[`2`,`Merchant has a pegasus mount (e188) for sale — 50 gold`],[`3`,`Merchant mentions old cave tombs in a random adjacent hex — if you go see e028`],[`4`,`Merchant has cure-poison vials (e181) for sale — 10 gold each`],[`5`,`Merchant mentions a farm nearby — if you investigate see e009`],[`6`,`Merchant has food for sale at 1 gold per 2 food units (max 8 units). Will also buy food from you giving 1 gold for 3 units`],[`7`,`Merchant may outwit you! Roll 1D6 — if exceeds W&W you spend 6 gold needlessly (or all your money if you have less)`],[`8`,`Merchant is an apothecary with healing potions (e180) for 5 gold each, poison drug (e185) for 10 gold and fine herbs that add +1 to Making an Offering (r212) for 5 gold`],[`9`,`Merchant has two horses for sale at 6 gold each and a wagon for 12 gold`],[`10`,`Merchant has a coffle of slaves for sale (e163). Also has a Gift of Charm (e182) for 8 gold`],[`11`,`Merchant provides final clues about a treasure — see e147`],[`12`,`Learn unique secrets from the merchant — see e162`]]}]},{tag:`e129`,title:`Merchant Caravan`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You meet a merchant caravan camped for the night. May halt for the day to talk and trade, try to rob the caravan, offer to sign on as a guard or ignore them. If you stop to talk: roll 2D6 and see below.`,note:`If caravan is on a road or habitation and you are alone: may get a job as a guard if you roll less than W&W on 1D6 — you get 3 gold per day and free food until reaching nearest habitation (1 hex/day) and fight with guards in any hostile encounters. If robbing the caravan: you strike first (r220). Caravan has 1D6+2 guards (each S5, E4, W4) all mounted on horses. If all guards killed: roll W60 twice for your spoils.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Trade Result (2D6)`,rows:[[`2`,`Learn unique secrets from caravan members — see e162`],[`3`,`Learn of a monastery in this hex — if you go see e066`],[`4`,`Merchants have an anti-poison amulet (e187) for sale — 25 gold`],[`5`,`Merchants noticed farms in this hex — if you go see e009`],[`6`,`Meet an independent merchant in the caravan — see e128`],[`7`,`Caravan guards become hostile and you must escape (r218)`],[`8`,`Caravan healer has healing potions (e180) for sale — 6 gold pieces each`],[`9`,`Caravan has up to six spare horses for sale — 7 gold pieces each`],[`10`,`Caravan has a coffle of slaves for sale — see e163`],[`11`,`Talk with caravan guards gives you hints to a treasure — see e147`],[`12`,`Caravan passed a nearby ruin (e064) yesterday (random adjacent hex)`]]}]},{tag:`e130`,title:`Meet a High Lord`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You meet a high and powerful lord of the Southlands. If at a habitation: it is the local lord. If not: roll 1D6 for identity. The Lord has a retinue of 2D6+1 guards (each S6, E6, W4).`,note:`If arrested: jailed at Lord's residence — appropriate castle, nearest temple for High Priest or nearest town for Mayor. Advance time by 1 day. If gaining audience: Baron Huldra→e152; Count Drogat→e161; Lady Aeravir→e160; High Priest→e155; Town Mayor→e156. May alternatively rob the Lord (wealth 100, or 110 if Count Drogat or Lady Aeravir) — if you do you can never enter the town/temple/castle hex where they rule without being arrested and sentenced to death (e061). Hirelings result: may bribe guards with 10 gold for an audience instead. If all guards killed while Lord watches: Lord must grant you an audience.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Lord Identity (1D6, if not at habitation)`,rows:[[`1`,`Baron Huldra of Huldra Castle`],[`2`,`Count Drogat of Drogat Castle`],[`3`,`Lady Aeravir of Aeravir Castle`],[`4`,`High Priest of a Temple`],[`5–6`,`Town Mayor`]]},{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`pass r327`,`escape mounted r312`,`surprise r303`],[`2`,`pass r329`,`escape r314`,`attack r305`],[`3`,`*arrested e060`,`escape r315`,`attacked r306`],[`4`,`**audience`,`hide r317`,`attacked r306`],[`5`,`***inquiry r342`,`hide r319`,`attacked r307`],[`6`,`attacked r307`,`attacked r306`,`surprised r309`]]}]},{tag:`e131`,title:`The Secret of Jakor's Keep`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`You learn that Jakor was a powerful wizard who lived many centuries ago. Legend has it that Jakor never really died and his spirit lives on in his decaying keep. The shade of Jakor might be able to help you regain your lost throne.`,note:`If you travel to Jakor's Keep (hex 0901) with a wizard/magician/witch in your party: after searching the ruins (r208) as an action you may try to invoke the Shade once only. Spending 40 gold at any town or castle buys a silver pentagram adding +1 to the roll. Destroying an unused resurrection necklace (e192) adds +2 to the roll.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Invocation Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Nothing happens — the legends were simply that...`],[`2`,`The ritual requires you sacrifice a life of one of your party. If you do, may roll again here but all remaining followers then desert you for being so cruel and callous`],[`3`,`The spectre of Jakor appears — see e034`],[`4`,`(Ψ) Jakor's spirit appears and listens to your tale before giving you a magical gift — see e195`],[`5+`,`The Shade of Jakor appears wreathed in flames and reveals that the ancestors of the usurpers were also responsible for his own death. He uses his magical powers to raise an undead army to retake the Northern Kingdom — you win the game`]]}]},{tag:`e132`,title:`Organized Search`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`The ruins have winding tunnels and caves twisting in many directions. Roll 1D6 — if the result is less than or equal to the number of characters in your party (any dwarf/elf/wizard/magician counts as two characters for this total): the organised search helps and you may roll on r208 again immediately. Otherwise you spend the day fruitlessly searching and find nothing today.`},{tag:`e133`,title:`Plague`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`After considerable searching you find items worth 50 gold among many skeletons. Tonight just before you start to eat (r215): a plague of mind-madness begins due to an ancient curse on this place. Roll 1D6 for each character in your party — on 3+ madness destroys their mind and they die foaming at the mouth. All surviving members immediately escape (r218) and camp elsewhere for the night (r215). Mounts are not affected so survivors can bring mounts, possessions and wealth of the entire party.`,note:`If you are a victim of the madness: your northern blood helps you survive. You wake the next morning having not eaten (r216) and find all your followers dead or escaped with your wealth and possessions.`},{tag:`e134`,title:`Unstable Ruins`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The ruins contain many unstable walls and rocks making your search very dangerous. May either give up searching and do nothing else today, or continue. If you continue: roll 1D6 for each character in your party — on 1 they are injured in the rubble, roll 2D6 for wounds suffered.`,note:`If any character survives the day unwounded: the search might yield results — see r208 and roll again. Repeat this process each day you search these ruins and must risk the rubble.`},{tag:`e135`,title:`Ancient Inscription`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Along a palisade of broken columns you find an altar with an ancient inscription. If your party includes a magician/wizard/witch/priest/monk: the inscription can be deciphered — roll 1D6 for what you discover. If none can decipher the inscription: there is no event.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Inscription Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e042 – Alcove of Sending`],[`2`,`e043 – Small Altar`],[`3`,`e044 – High Altar`],[`4`,`e045 – Arch of Travel`],[`5`,`e046 – Gateway to Darkness`],[`6`,`e047 – Mirror of Reversal`]]}]},{tag:`e136`,title:`Hidden Treasures`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`ou uncover the remains of an old and decaying palace treasure room, its contents undiscovered for many years. Roll 1D6 and if the result is lower than your Wit & Wiles, see e136a, otherwise roll 1D6 for the contents:`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:``,rows:[[`1`,`e037`],[`2`,`e038`],[`3`,`e039`],[`4`,`e044`],[`5`,`500 gold`],[`6`,`Nothing`]]}]},{tag:`e136a`,title:`Northland Banners`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You notice that the banners within the treasure room match those of your royal House of Arath! Roll 1D6 for what you discover:`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:``,rows:[[`1`,`Memories of ancient and noble battles (Ψ)`],[`2-3`,`e034 (with +1 to 1D6 roll)`],[`4`,`e186a`],[`5`,`e194`],[`6`,`A shade appears before you– see e200.`]]}]},{tag:`e137`,title:`Inhabitants`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You encounter things living in the ruins. Roll 1D6 for what you find.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Inhabitants (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e032 – Ghosts`],[`2`,`e051 – Bandits`],[`3`,`e052 – Goblins`],[`4`,`e055 – Orcs`],[`5`,`e057 – Troll`],[`6`,`e082 – Spectre`]]}]},{tag:`e138`,title:`Unclean`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`As you explore the ruins you discover they are unclean with the foul stench of decay and dangerous gruesome creatures populating them. Roll 1D6 for what you encounter. If you survive the encounter: may immediately consult r208 again to continue your search or stop searching for the day and eat your evening meal (r215).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Creature (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e032 – Ghosts`],[`2`,`e033 – Warrior Wraiths`],[`3`,`e034 – Spectre of the Inner Tomb`],[`4`,`e056 – Orc Tower`],[`5`,`e082 – Spectre`],[`6`,`e098 – Dragon`]]}]},{tag:`e139`,title:`Minor Treasure`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`In a small dusty room with only a glimmer of light through a crack in the roof you uncover a minor treasure. Roll 1D6 for what you find.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Contents (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Wealth 25`],[`2`,`Wealth 60`],[`3`,`e038 – Cache under Stone`],[`4`,`e039 – Treasure Chest`],[`5`,`e140 – Magic Box`],[`6`,`e142 – Precious Gems`]]}]},{tag:`e140`,title:`Magic Box`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You find a strange magic box inscribed with ancient runes and symbols. Can only be opened if you have a magician/wizard/witch in your party or if you gain an audience with a wizard (e068b). Until then you can carry it with you as it is relatively light. Once such a person is in your party you can open it — roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Contents (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e141 – Hydra's Teeth`],[`2`,`e142 – Precious Gems`],[`3`,`Wealth 60`],[`4`,`Wealth 110`],[`5`,`e195 – Magical Possession`],[`6`,`Just rubbish`]]}]},{tag:`e141`,title:`Hydra's Teeth`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`Roll 2D6 for the number of teeth inside. The magician/wizard/witch explains that whenever you scatter these teeth on the ground that number of undead skeleton warriors will rise up and fight in your party for one combat (r220) at your command. These skeleton warriors are S5, E4* but will only last for that combat and then disappear.`,note:`You can scatter the teeth at any instant including at the start or during any combat and may use as many teeth as you wish each time.`},{tag:`e142`,title:`Precious Gems`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`The box contains a pouch of gems total worth wealth 200 (consult for wealth 100 twice). There are 1D6+2 gems — divide up the total wealth by this number to find out how much each gem is worth (round down).`,note:`If you have a magician/wizard/witch in your party: they also recognise one as a vision gem — see e041.`},{tag:`e143`,title:`Secret of the Temples`,type:`story`,image:!1,desc:`You learn the secret of all temple priests — the Chaga drug. This is available in any town where you buy food for 2 gold pieces a serving.`,note:`If you buy a serving and offer it to a temple priest while Making an Offering (r212): add +1 to the roll. If you give it to temple priests when you Seek an Audience with the High Priest (r211): add +1 to that roll instead.`},{tag:`e144`,title:`The Secret of Baron Huldra`,type:`story`,image:!1,desc:`You learn that the Baron of Huldra is actually a double bastard, and has imprisoned the true heir to the castle with a ‘Tribe of the Bear’ hill clan in the Wredrock Badlands (hex 1611). You can attempt to rescue the heir and install him as the rightful Baron at Huldra if you wish. To do so, you must travel to hex 1611 and defeat the hill tribe. This requires that you have a magician, wizard or witch in your party (to cast a spell); that you have a magic sword (e186) or shield of light (e193) to impress the tribesmen, or a charisma talisman (e189) or wear the pelt of a bear as a cloak (e084) to walk among them, or a death mist potion (e190) to kill them. Alternatively, you can simply fight through the guards of the heir, making a surprise attack in combat (r220) on them, roll 3D6+1 for the number of guards, each of which is S4, E5, W2; you then escape (r218) from the hex with the true heir who is S5, E7, W0. You must then travel with the heir, and if you reach Huldra Castle, your party and the heir have two options. First, you can seek an audience with the current Baron (r211), and if you roll a ‘10’ or higher (after any modifiers) you can reveal the true heir at the audience and depose the current Baron instantly. Alternatively, you can make a sneak attack on the Baron at night, surprising in combat (r220) his traditional six bodyguards, each of which is S6, E6, W4 and then go on to attack the imposter Baron, who is S6, E8, and who strikes first in combat.`,note:`If you succeed in disposing of the original Baron, the heir will take the throne and will return the favour by marching his army with you back to the Northlands, to help you regain your throne. You have won the game, due to the aid of the new Baron Huldra!`},{tag:`e145`,title:`The Secret of Lady Aeravir`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`The Lady of Aeravir Castle is the priestess of a local temple cult that requires virginity. You discover that she is actually quite promiscuous and can use this to your advantage if you gain an audience with her. Add +1 when attempting to gain an audience (r211) with her and if successful, you can roll twice for the results of the audience and choose either result.`,note:`However, the Lady dislikes being blackmailed, and you must escape from the hex (r218a) at the end of the day when you use this secret knowledge. You can then never return to Aeravir castle.`},{tag:`e146`,title:`The Secret of Count Drogat`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`You learn that Count Drogat, Lord of Drogat Castle, is actually an undead creature who lives from the suffering, pain, and death of others. This explains why he so often tortures and dismembers felons in his realm, and often goes into rages that leave his lands in a reign of terror. However, he is very vulnerable to foulbane, a rare plant which can only be purchased from the druids at Duffyd Temple (hex 2018) for 1 gold piece. Using the foulbane, when you try to gain an audience (r211) with Count Drogat add +1 to your roll, and if you do gain an audience may ignore the first audience result die roll and try again. However, you must abide by the second result.`,note:`Finally, you can use the foulbane in Drogat Castle to help rob the Count of his personal jewels - see r224b, but add +2 to your Wit & Wiles for the roll. If you fail the roll, you are confronted by 1D6+1 skeleton guards (each S5, E4*). If you pass the Wit & Wiles roll or kill the skeletons, you escape from the hex (r218) with wealth 110, but can never return to the castle hex (due to the Count's anger).`},{tag:`e147`,title:`Clue to Treasure`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You gain information about a nearby treasure. Roll 1D6 for a random direction then roll 1D6 for the distance in hexes. When you reach that hex and spend a day searching for it like a cache (r214): roll 2D6 to see what you find.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Treasure Found (2D6)`,rows:[[`2`,`e066 – Secret Temple`],[`3`,`e037 – Broken Chest`],[`4`,`e038 – Cache under Stone`],[`5`,`e039 – Treasure Chest`],[`6`,`e040 – Trapped Chest`],[`7`,`e030 – Mummies`],[`8`,`Wealth 110`],[`9`,`e139 – Minor Treasure`],[`10`,`e140 – Magic Box`],[`11`,`e136 – Hidden Treasures`],[`12`,`e054 – Goblin Keep`]]}]},{tag:`e148`,title:`Seneschal Requires Bribe`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You must pay a bribe to the seneschal to gain an audience with the Lord. Roll 1D6×5 for the number of gold pieces needed in the bribe.`,note:`If you pay this bribe: add +5 and roll for seeking an audience (r211) once more. If you don't pay the bribe: you cannot attempt an audience with the Lord in this hex for the rest of the game.`},{tag:`e149`,title:`Learn Court Manners`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`Your northern ways brand you as a boor. You are turned away as un-presentable. After you spend 10 gold pieces for better clothes in any town or castle you can try again for an audience. Until then you cannot seek any further audiences in this hex.`,note:`Once you have spent for better clothes this event can still occur again indicating that you must spend yet more to improve your appearance!`},{tag:`e150`,title:`Pay Your Respects`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You gain an audience with the Lord or Lady, pay your respects, tell your tales and receive a purse of wealth 50.`,note:`You cannot seek an audience with that same lord tomorrow but after that could try again.`},{tag:`e151`,title:`Find Favour`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You gain an audience and are heard favourably. Your entire party is given food (r215) and lodging (r217) free tonight. Tomorrow you are given a gift of gold — roll 1D6 and multiply by 100 for the amount — plus an escort of cavalry that guides you and remains with you during the day you leave the hex.`,note:`The escort is so strong that you will automatically defeat and kill anything you encounter.`},{tag:`e152`,title:`Audience with Baron Huldra`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You are allowed a semi-private interview with the ruler of Huldra Castle. Roll 1D6 for the result, adding +1 to the result if you are wearing a bear-pelt cloak:`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:``,rows:[[`1`,`The Baron flies into a surprising rage and imprisons you in the dungeon — see e062. Roll 1D6: on 6 see e144. Cannot seek another audience with him`],[`2`,`He listens but seems distracted — audience ends without result but you can seek an audience again some other day`],[`3`,`He seems sympathetic to your cause and gives you a gift of wealth 50 to help in your quest but decrees you cannot seek another audience with him`],[`4`,`The Baron agrees you have been unfairly usurped. You and your entire party can eat (r215) and lodge (r217) at his castle for as long as you wish. He also provides one of his knights (S7, E6, W15) and horses for you all. Cannot seek another audience with him`],[`5`,`If you have dismissed a magician/wizard/witch to take service with the Baron: he provides free food (r215) and lodging (r217) tonight and gives you wealth 110. If not: he dismisses you with just a Letter of Recommendation (e157) to a habitation of your choice`],[`6+`,`You gain an audience and are heard with interest. The Baron decides to support your cause fully and prepares to march his army to the Northlands with you to help you regain your throne. You have fulfilled your quest and won the game!`]]}]},{tag:`e153`,title:`Master of the Household`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You encounter the Master of the Household, who prevents you from receiving an audience. If you bribe him, you can try for an audience on some future day. Otherwise, he takes a dislike to you, and you can never attempt to seek an audience (r211) in this hex again. The bribe is 6 gold if in a village or 10 gold if in a town or castle. In a temple, you must instead ‘donate’ 5 gold to the needy. In a halfling village (e070), the Master just requires 1 food unit!`},{tag:`e154`,title:`Meet Lord's Daughter`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You meet the ruler's beautiful daughter. Roll 1D6 for her attitude.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Daughter's Attitude (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`She hates you — see e060. Cannot seek an audience here again`],[`3`,`You try to dally with her but she is reserved. Attempt to seek an audience ends with no result but you can try again another day`],[`4`,`She is very reserved so you bow and pass on — roll again immediately for seeking an audience (r211)`],[`5`,`She takes a liking to you and this helps you — roll again immediately for seeking an audience (r211) and add +1 to your roll`],[`6`,`She falls in love with you (r228) — roll again immediately for seeking an audience (r211) and add +4 to your roll. If you leave the hex she will leave with you. You will be accused of kidnapping and are wanted throughout the land as if you had killed someone in every town/temple/castle south of the Tragoth River. However your new lover (S2, E2) has acquired horses for your entire party and brings her personal jewellery worth wealth 200 (consult wealth 100 twice, r225)`]]}]},{tag:`e155`,title:`Audience with High Priest`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You have a private discussion with the wise high priest of the temple. Roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Audience Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`He is insulted — see e060 immediately`],[`2`,`He hears your pleas but remains unmoved by your difficulties. Event ends and cannot seek another audience here until next week`],[`3`,`He hears your pleas and suggests you try offerings at the temple (r212) tomorrow or on some later day. Cannot seek another audience in this hex until you make this offering. If you have made an offering at a temple within the last three days: roll again with +1 to the roll`],[`4`,`He listens and offers to help you with your offerings at the temple tomorrow (r212). If you agree and make the offerings tomorrow: add +2 to your roll when resolving that action. Cannot seek another audience in this hex until you make an offering here`],[`5`,`He decides to provide modest support for your quest — gives you wealth 110 (r225) and heals all your wounds (but not other characters in your party). Cannot seek an audience again in this hex`],[`6+`,`He decides to provide full support for your quest and adventures. He gives you 200 in gold plus wealth 110. Cannot seek an audience again here`]]}]},{tag:`e156`,title:`Audience with Town Mayor`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You have private discussions with the mayor of the town. Roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Audience Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`He is insulted — see e060 immediately`],[`2`,`He hears your story but remains stone-faced and is either unwilling or unable to help. You are free to seek audiences with him again`],[`3`,`He hears your story and gives you free food and lodging for tonight as a distinguished if dispossessed visitor from the north. You are free to seek audiences with him again on a future day`],[`4`,`He hears your pleas with favour and gives you a Letter of Recommendation (e157) to the Lord of the nearest castle. Cannot seek another audience with him until next week`],[`5`,`He gives you a Letter of Recommendation (e157) to the Lord of the nearest castle and a purse of 50 gold. Cannot seek another audience with him until you have used the letter`],[`6+`,`If your party includes a monk or priest: he will support your cause for religious reasons — provides Letter of Recommendation (e157) to any castle or temple you request, gives you wealth 100 and his trusted assistant joins your party (S4, E4, W4). Cannot seek another audience until letter is used or assistant leaves. If no monk/priest: dismisses you and refuses further audiences until you have one`]]}]},{tag:`e157`,title:`Letter of Recommendation`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`You are given a properly signed and sealed letter that provides an introduction to the appropriate Lord, Elder, High Priest or Mayor. This allows you to add +2 to your roll when seeking an audience (r211) with that Lord in their hex of residence.`},{tag:`e158`,title:`Hostile Guards`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`You leave the rest of your party in an atrium and are then confronted by two hostile guards (each S5, E6, W7). If you pay 20 gold as a bribe they will let you pass and you can roll again on the appropriate seeking audience table (r211). Otherwise they will attack you getting the first strike in combat (r220).`,note:`Regardless of whether you win or lose: you must immediately escape from this hex (r218) but can return later in the game.`},{tag:`e159`,title:`Must Purify Yourself`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You are deemed unworthy and must make an offering at a temple (r212) before you can attempt another audience (r211) in this hex. The offering can be made at any temple. This result does not prevent you from attempting audiences elsewhere before making this offering.`,note:`If you do make the required offering and then try for another audience in this hex: your devotion is noted — add +2 to roll when seeking the audience (r211).`},{tag:`e160`,title:`Audience with Lady Aeravir`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You are allowed a semi-private interview with the ruler of Aeravir Castle. Roll 1D6 for the result.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Audience Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`She listens graciously but has no interest in the Northlands. Cannot seek another audience with her. However if you have a lover with you (r228): she suspects the Lady is not what she appears — see e145`],[`2`,`She listens but seems distracted — audience ends without result but you can seek an audience again some other day`],[`3`,`She takes pity on you and gives you a gift of wealth 60 to help in your quest but decrees you cannot seek another audience with her`],[`4`,`She finds you favourably endowed with virtue and is a gracious host. You and your entire party can eat (r215) and lodge (r217) at her castle for as long as you wish whenever you wish. She also provides a gift of wealth 110. Cannot seek another audience with her`],[`5`,`She has seductive charms! Roll 1D6 for the number of days that pass before you come to your senses again. Then roll 1D6 on this table again. Meantime your entire party has been living in the castle but any true love (r228) has deserted in despair and you cannot roll for her return until after you leave this hex`],[`6+`,`The Lady decides to support your cause fully. She gives you 1D6×150 in gold, an escort of three stalwart knights (each S7, E6), horses for your entire party and a spare pack-horse. Tonight she will hold a grand feast providing food (r215) and lodging (r217) free. If any party members have wounds: her healers will cure all wounds including poisoned wounds`]]}]},{tag:`e161`,title:`Audience with Count Drogat`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You are allowed a semi-private interview with the fearsome ruler of Drogat Castle. Roll 1D6 for the result, adding +1 if you have a Troll skin and give it to the Count.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Audience Result (1D6, +1 with Troll skin)`,rows:[[`1`,`The Count's eyes glow like red coals — you are to be his next victim, see e061! Roll 1D6: on 6 see e146. Cannot enter this hex again for the rest of the game`],[`2`,`Count Drogat listens with half an ear — audience ends with no result but you can seek another audience some other day`],[`3`,`The Count is in a humorous mood, gives you flippant advice and sends you forth. Must leave Drogat Castle tomorrow and advised never to seek an audience again unless you carry a Letter of Recommendation (e157)`],[`4`,`Count Drogat takes an unusual interest in your situation and provides you with 100 gold and a treasure worth wealth 110 to further your cause`],[`5`,`If you have killed (personally) at least five men or creatures: the Count takes an interest in you and provides 300 gold, a treasure of wealth 110 and two winged pegasus mounts. Advised never to seek an audience again. If not five kills yet: dismissed and cannot seek audience again until you have killed five`],[`6+`,`The Count listens with interest. Upon learning the names of the northern usurpers he declares that they were the very ones who did him ill deeds many years ago. He immediately rallies his army to your cause and uses his powerful magic to transport you and his army to the Northland capital to retake your throne. You immediately win`]]}]},{tag:`e162`,title:`Learn Secrets`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You finally accumulate enough hints and bits of unrelated information to learn of the important secrets of this region. Roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Secret Learned (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e147 – Clue to Treasure`],[`2`,`e143 – Secret of the Temples`],[`3`,`e176 – The Secret of the Ruins`],[`4`,`e144 – The Secret of Baron Huldra`],[`5`,`e145 – The Secret of Lady Aeravir`],[`6`,`e146 – The Secret of Count Drogat`]]}]},{tag:`e163`,title:`Slave Market`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You see slaves at the auction block. You can purchase some (see below), or attempt to free them by attacking the slavers (r303). There are 5 guards (each S5, E4, W4), led by a despicable, fat hunchback (S7, E6, W70). If you kill the slavers and free the slaves, you will be a ‘wanted’ man here if at a habitation. Porter slaves (see r206) are available at 1D6 gold per porter, and each S1, E2. These slaves need not be paid wages, and will function even if not fed (r215), but each day without food reduces their carrying capacity by 2 as normal. When their capacity reaches zero they die. Slave girls (each S1, E2) are available at 2D6 gold each. A slave girl functions as a Gift of Charm (e182) and can be given as such, as long as she is fed (r215). Slave girls who are not fed lose the ability until they are fed for as many days as they missed meals (and each day without food for the girl, normal starvation risks apply). Finally, for each girl bought, roll 2D6. If the result is ‘12’ you fall in love and free her, see r228. Finally, you find an old warrior. Only you spot his hidden qualities. Roll 1D6 to establish the price in gold. Add +2 to this price if you didn't buy any porters or slave girls. If you buy him, he becomes a willing member of your party at no pay as soon as you free him. Roll 1D6+1 for his Skill and again for Endurance.`},{tag:`e164`,title:`Giant Lizard`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`A huge lizard that shakes the earth as it walks attacks you with a deafening roar. It is S10, E12. You strike first in combat (r220) but do not gain any mounted bonus (r220h) to your attacking rolls. Ranged combat (r220i) only has a -2 modifier.`,note:`Escape is only possible if you have mounts (r312) or can fly (r313) — those without mounts or the ability to fly cannot escape as the lizard is too fast.`},{tag:`e165`,title:`The Imp of Pelgar`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`You learn that the town of Pelgar was destroyed by an evil imp after the townsfolk reneged on a bargain struck with the infernal creature. The Imp is rumoured to still inhabit the ruins torturing the souls of the dead. Perhaps you can strike a deal with the Imp to reclaim your kingdom?`,note:`If you travel to the Ruins of Pelgar (hex 2009) with a wizard/magician/witch/priest/monk in your party or a resistance talisman (e184) to protect you from the Imp's magic: after searching the ruins as a daily action (r208) roll 1D6. Magic comes at a price — if you accept the Imp's offer: roll 1D6 again to discover the cost. If you decline or cannot pay: Imp vanishes with a snarl and curses you — lower either Skill/Endurance/W&W by 1 until the curse is broken by a True Love (r228).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Search Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Nothing happens — the legend was simply a story to scare small children...`],[`2`,`The Imp appears. Roll 1D6: on 1–3 the magical protection is not enough and you are doomed — game over. On 4–6 the Imp is taken aback by your magic and agrees to listen — see result 4–6`],[`3`,`The Imp appears and attacks with its magic. It has S4, E3, W110 but is only hurt by wounds from a magic sword or attacks by a priest/monk/magician/wizard/witch`],[`4–6`,`The cackling Imp appears with a bow. Roll 1D6 for what it offers: 1–2→e195; 3–4→1D6×100 gold; 5→making any female character in your party or one you later meet your True Love (r228); 6→the return of your throne and instantly winning the game`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Cost of Imp's Offer (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`A magical treasure (any one of e183–e194)`],[`3–4`,`The location of a magical device (any one of e042–e047)`],[`5`,`The sacrifice of a True Love (r228)`],[`6`,`Your immortal soul — you lose the game (even if you retook the throne!)`]]}]},{tag:`e166`,title:`Elf Haven`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`[Site] You discover an enchanted, hidden castle inhabited by elves. Roll 1D6 with -1 to the roll if your party includes an elf, druid, magician, wizard, or witch, and +1 if your party includes a dwarf. If the roll equals or exceeds your Wit & Wiles, the elves decide you are unworthy, and your entire party including yourself are arrested see e060. Otherwise, you may visit the Haven. If you can visit, treat the Haven like a castle marked on the map for all purposes, including daily actions (r203). Food (r215) and lodging (r217) are free if you have an elf in your party. Due to the reclusive nature of the Haven, you cannot Seek News (r209) and there is no Tavern (r230). You can Study (r229) here (with +1 to the roll). Any followers hired here (r210) will be elves. You may also try to Seek an Audience (r211l) with the rather indifferent Elf King who rules here. If you fight elves here, you must escape the Haven (r218).`,note:`If you return to the Haven later in the game, you must roll again to see if the elves still give you permission to visit or arrest you (see above). They will always arrest you if you entered combat with any elves last time you were at the Haven (see e060 with -1 to roll).`},{tag:`e166a`,title:`Audience with Elf King`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`You have a private discussion with the King of the woodland realm. You may not attempt to seek an audience here again. Roll 1D6 (add +1 to roll if you have an elf in your party):`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:``,rows:[[`1`,`The Elf King is angry that your coming may have alerted the outside world to the hidden Haven — you are imprisoned in the dungeons (e062)`],[`2`,`The Elf King is unmoved by your story — the world of men is of no concern to him. You must leave the Elf Haven tomorrow`],[`3`,`The Elf King listens and offers advice but it is so obtuse that unless you roll 1D6 less than your W&W score you learn nothing. If you succeed: roll 1D6 — 1–3→e147; 4–6→e176. Party fed and lodged free tonight`],[`4`,`The Elf King agrees to help and pronounces you an Elf-Friend. Gives you W110 and a clasp recognised by elves throughout the land — may roll twice on any Talk option with elves and choose the result. Party fed and lodged free tonight`],[`5`,`The Elf King shares some ancient magic. Roll 1D6: 1–2→e041; 3–4→e045 (use as if wizard in party); 5–6→e198 (in random adjacent forest hex). Party fed and lodged free tonight`],[`6`,`The Elf King agrees to help with your quest. Party fed and lodged tonight. He bids his son the Elf Prince (S6, E6, W12, attacks twice per round in ranged combat r220i) join you as an ally. The King also gives each character in your party an elf cloak making any Hide action (r317–r320) in a forest hex always successful`],[`7+`,`The Elf King becomes your noble ally, musters an army of woodland archers and warriors and marches them North to regain your throne. You have fulfilled your quest and won the game!`]]}]},{tag:`e167`,title:`The Warrior's Heart`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`You learn the ruins of the Dead Plains are of an ancient civilization that might contain a legendary artifact named ‘The Warrior’s Heart’. It is said that any army that carries the Heart is so mighty it can win any battle. Could this be the way of reclaiming your throne? If you travel to The Dead Plains Ruins (hex 0206), as a daily action (r203) you can enter the inner tombs by either your party having a total Skill of 20 or more to remove heavy columns from the entrance, or find another way in rolling 1D6 less than your Wit & Wiles score. Once inside you find yourself in a massive, arched hallway lit dimly by burning torches. Several arched doorways lead from it and into darkness... To explore the ruins as a daily action (r204), roll 2D6 (with +1 to the roll if there is a dwarf or wizard in your party, +2 if both, +1 if you made the Wit & Wiles roll above):`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Ruins Exploration (2D6)`,rows:[[`2–3`,`Lost in the Dark — cannot find your way out, must end the day underground, cannot hunt (r215b). Tomorrow continue exploring with -1 to roll. If rolled again: still trapped. Any other result means you can exit after any event ends`],[`4`,`Endless Tunnels — cannot continue exploring tomorrow unless you roll 1D6 less than or equal to W&W or have a Dwarf in your party`],[`5`,`Earth Tremors — roll 1D6 (+1 each time previously rolled here): 1–3 tremors subside; 4–5 see e088; 6 a chasm opens — roll 1D6 for each character, on 1 they plunge to their death. All further rolls exploring these ruins have a -2 (cumulative) penalty`],[`6`,`Traps — see r227 and apply to a random character (r343)`],[`7`,`Clues to Treasure — if you roll 1D6 less than or equal to your W&W score you gain +1 to all further rolls exploring these ruins (cumulative)`],[`8`,`Secret Passage — if you roll 1D6 less than or equal to W&W or have a Dwarf in your party: gain +2 to your next (only) roll exploring these ruins`],[`9`,`Lost Treasures — roll 1D6: 1–2→e038; 3–4→e039; 5–6→e040`],[`10`,`Ruined Crypt — roll 1D6: 1–2→e036; 3–4→e170; 5–6→e135`],[`11`,`Important Discovery — roll 1D6: 1–3→e136; 4–6→e139`],[`12+`,`You have reached the innermost part of the ruins where the fabled Heart rests. Must cross a chasm using only a tiny stone ledge — roll 1D6 twice. If either result is 1: you fall to your death. If you survive: you retrieve the Warrior's Heart (Ψ)`]]}]},{tag:`e168`,title:`Roadside Inn`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You have come across a small but bustling inn at the side of the road. Food (r215e) and lodging (r217) are available. Roll 1D6 — on 4+ the inn also has stables for mounts. May stay quiet in your rooms or Visit the Tavern (r230) here. May also try for a job (r233 — use village line).`,note:`The innkeeper will also sell you food for 2 units per gold piece when you leave. If the inn has stables: there will also be 1D3-1 horses for sale costing 7 gold each.`},{tag:`e169`,title:`Festival`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`A jolly festival is being held here. No food cost today for your party as it is free in the streets. Any lodging (r217) costs double due to the large crowds. No other actions (r203) are available today. Roll 1D6 for a further event.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Festival Event (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Minstrel — see e049`],[`2`,`Thieves — see e173`],[`3`,`Merchant — see e128`],[`4`,`Find a busy tavern — roll on Visit Tavern (r230) with +1 to the roll`],[`5`,`You hear lots of news — roll twice on Seek News & Information (r209)`],[`6`,`A tournament is taking place — see e179`]]}]},{tag:`e170`,title:`Vampire`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Postponed until after evening meal (r215). As a cloud of mist solidifies into a shape you realise you have encountered one of the most terrifying creatures of the night — the vampire! Roll 1D6: on 1–3 a male vampire (†S8, E8*, W60); on 4–6 a female vampire (†S7, E7*, W25). See r306. Vampires can only be harmed by magical weapons, spells or by a priest. Vampire is -1S if there is a priest in your party or if you have any foulbane plant (-2 if both).`,note:`At start of combat: vampire uses mesmerism — you must roll less than W&W (less than or equal if male vampire) and each other character must roll 5–6 (4–6 if male vampire) to be able to attack rather than being frozen in fear. If vampire ever reduced to less than half its Endurance: roll 1D6 at end of a combat round — on 4–6 the vampire turns into a bat and flies away. If vampire killed: its cape and clasp bear the insignia of Drogat Castle — if you Seek an Audience (r211) with Count Drogat and give him the cloak add +2 to the die roll.`},{tag:`e171`,title:`Werewolf`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Postponed until after evening meal (r215). As the full moon shines balefully down you hear the snarl of a huge wolf emerging from the shadows. You are surprised (r308) by the werewolf (†S7, E7) which can only be harmed by magical or holy weapons — or by Bonebiter! It has also summoned 1D6-2 hungry wolves (each S3, E3). May only escape (r220e) on 5–6 (6 only if there are wolves).`,note:`If a character is bitten by a werewolf: roll 1D6 for each wound — on 5–6 the character is infected by lycanthropy and will turn into a werewolf in exactly 4 weeks time (unless in a temple). If you transform: you have lost the game. If werewolf killed: it changes back into a poor man. If you take the corpse back to a temple to be properly buried: gain +3 to your next Seek Audience (r211) or Make Offering (r212) action there. To cure lycanthropy: must gain a successful audience with a High Priest (r211) — instead of e155 you learn that you must spend the full moon on a mountain hex chained in silver (available from any castle at 30 gold). If a character does this they are no longer a werewolf.`},{tag:`e172`,title:`Gypsy`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You have met a gypsy. Roll 1D6: on 1–3 a male rogue (S5, E4, W7); on 4–6 a female dancer (S3, E4, W5). Roll 1D6 to see if the gypsy is alone: on 1–3 alone; on 4 you have encountered a brother and sister (encounter both); on 5–6 the gypsy is part of a gypsy caravan (see e172b for additional gypsies).`,note:`*Thief – the gypsy attempts to pick your pocket. Roll 1D6: If you roll less than your Wit & Wiles, you catch them in the attempt – see r305. If you roll greater than your Wit & Wiles, lose half your wealth. ** If your party has mounts, you may use escape mounted (r312) instead of rolling for an evade option. If the gypsy joins your party you may also opt to have your fortune told (see e172a, once only) and you gain +1 to your Wit & Wiles when selecting a ‘Thievery’ action (r224). Finally, if a gypsy dancer joins your party, roll 1D6 and on a 5-6 you become lovers (r228).`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`conversation r341`,`escape r311`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`conversation r341`,`escape r314`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`inquiry r342`,`escape r315`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`fortune e172a`,`hide r319`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`thief*`,`thief*`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`attacked r306`,`hide r317`,`battle r330`]]}]},{tag:`e172a`,title:`Fortune-teller`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`The gypsy offers to tell your fortune in a crystal ball for 5 gold. If you accept, roll 1D6:`,note:`After the reading, you may choose to roll on the Talk option on e172 again, or end the event.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Fortune Telling (1D6, costs 5 gold)`,rows:[[`1`,`Death! All your followers immediately desert you!`],[`2`,`Meaningless nonsense — no further event`],[`3`,`Wealth! You may roll twice on any one future Wealth roll of your choice`],[`4`,`Treasure! The gypsy tells of clues to possible treasure — see e147`],[`5`,`Love! You may choose a female character in your party or one you later encounter to become your lover (r228)`],[`6`,`Destiny! Told of legends entwined with your own fate — see e176 (Ψ)`]]}]},{tag:`e172b`,title:`Gypsy Caravan`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`The gypsies are part of a caravan of colored wagons. Roll 1D3+1 for the number of caravans, each containing 1D3 additional gypsies (each S4, E4, W4). If the above encounter does not end incombat, you may join the caravan. If at a habitation, roll 1D6 and on 1-3, the gypsies are part of a carnival (see e199) for the next 1D6 days. After this time, or if you have encountered the caravan on the road, roll randomly the road direction they are travelling at a speed of 1hex/day. If you have a travel encounter (r207), the gypsies will fight alongside your party. At the end of each day, roll 1D6 and on a 6, see e053. Gypsy tradition means that they will share their food with you first, and then you must provide food for them, on alternate days. If you will not or cannot, then you must leave the caravan or they attack you (r306).`,note:`If you reach a habitation (except a temple) roll 1D6 again (as above) to see if the gypsies stay and put on a carnival (e199) for a few days. You may decide to leave the caravan at any time, either on the road or at a habitation.`},{tag:`e173`,title:`Thieves`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You have been set upon by 1D3+1 (+2 if in a town) thieves (each S4, E4, W15). Roll 1D6 and add your W&W (-1 if thieves outnumber your party, -1 if you attempted thievery r224 in this hex, -1 if that action was against a thief or the thieves' guild).`,note:`If result is 5 or less: you are ambushed (r308). If 6+: thieves allow you to surrender and will not attack if you give them all your possessions and gold. If you refuse: you are attacked (r306). In combat: thieves rout (r220f) on 5–6. If in a habitation: may call out at end of a normal combat round — roll 1D6, on 5+ then 1D3 guards come to help (each S5, E4, W4) and thieves rout on 4–6. If you survive the attack without aid from guards and knock a thief unconscious: can extract location of the thieves' guild in the nearest town. May attempt to rob the guild within the next 3 days (r224a, Wealth 70, +1 to number of guards) or try to kill the Head of the Guild (S6, E5, W70) with 2D6 thieves (each S4, E4, W15) and then become Head yourself earning 3D6 gold each day you remain in town. At end of each day as Head: roll 1D6 — on 1 see e050.`},{tag:`e174`,title:`Holy Chapel`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`You have found a small stone chapel maintained by a small order of priests. Free food (r215) and lodging (r217) if you are alone or have just 1 more person in your party and have less than 10 gold pieces. If not: food and lodging at half normal price. No stables. Any night spent at the chapel heals 1 wound (r222). May Make an Offering (r212) here but any result of 11+ counts as 10.`,note:`May easily steal from the chapel — if you do: you will have the Unholy Mark (see e018) and will be a wanted man here (see e050). Roll 1D6 for wealth: 1–2→10; 3→15; 4→e180; 5→30; 6→50.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Additional Event (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e032 – Ghosts`],[`2–3`,`e069 – Wounded Warrior`],[`4`,`e018 – Priest`],[`5–6`,`No further event`]]}]},{tag:`e175`,title:`Apothecary`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You have found the shop of an apothecary. Roll 1D6 to find out what he has for sale. He will also buy any of the above potions for half their listed price. When you drink any potions bought from the apothecary: roll 1D6 — on 1 the potion fails and does nothing (probably).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Stock (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Healing potions (e180) at 6 gold each`],[`2`,`Cure Poison Vials (e181) at 10 gold each`],[`3`,`Poison Drugs (e185) at 8 gold each`],[`4`,`One Death Mist Potion (e190) at 20 gold`],[`5`,`One Wisdom Potion at 30 gold — gives +1 to W&W and knowledge of a priest`],[`6`,`One Flying Potion at 12 gold — allows airborne travel (r204d) for 1 day`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Other Customers (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`You are alone`],[`3`,`e128 – Merchant`],[`4`,`e073 – Witch`],[`5`,`e023 – Wizard`],[`6`,`e020 – Traveling Monk`]]}]},{tag:`e176`,title:`The Secret of the Ruins`,type:`story`,image:!1,desc:`From dusty scrolls or whispers in towers you have learnt about some of the ancient legends of this land that concern the ruins of a forgotten age. Roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Secret (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`e131 – The Secret of Jakor's Keep`],[`3–4`,`e165 – The Imp of Pelgar`],[`5–6`,`e167 – The Warrior's Heart`]]}]},{tag:`e177`,title:`Audience with Village Elder`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You have a mostly private discussion with the wise elder of the village. Roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Audience Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`He is insulted. If you are in Weshor (hex 1601): see e002. Otherwise see e060 (+1 to roll)`],[`2`,`He hears your story but remains stone-faced and is either unwilling or unable to help. You are free to seek audiences with him again`],[`3`,`He hears your story and gives you free food and lodging for tonight as a distinguished if dispossessed visitor from the north. You are free to seek audiences with him again on another day`],[`4`,`He hears your pleas favourably giving you a Letter of Recommendation (e157) to the Mayor of the nearest town. Cannot seek another audience with him until next week`],[`5`,`He hears your story with interest, gives you a Letter of Recommendation (e157) to the Mayor of the nearest town and a purse of 30 gold. Cannot seek another audience with him until you have used the Letter`],[`6+`,`The Elder decides to help you and will provide horses for each of your party, up to 10 food units and wealth 50. Roll 1D6: 1–2→his eldest son (S4, E4, W4) joins as a member of your party; 3–4→he tells you of a magician that lives in the village who may help you (if you visit see e016); 5–6→the Elder provides nothing further. Cannot seek another audience with the Elder again`]]}]},{tag:`e178`,title:`Dryad`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter a dryad or wood nymph (S4, E6, W5). Roll 1D6 (-1 to the roll if you have a druid or elf in your party) — if result exceeds W&W she is hostile; if equal she ignores you and event ends; if less she is friendly. Then roll 1D6 to determine her action.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Hostile Dryad Action (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`The dryad attacks calling upon the forest to aid her — roll for 1D6 roots and vines (each S3, E3*) and see r306. Character cannot escape unless roots attacking them have been overcome`],[`4–6`,`The dryad attempts to beguile you. Roll 1D6 for each character in the party — on 6 the character is charmed by the dryad's spell. Un-beguiled characters may attack the dryad (r304) or escape (r311) but if they do any beguiled characters are lost. If you are beguiled with no one to help you: you lose the game. Beguiled characters can be restored by spells of a magician/wizard/witch/druid or if the dryad is killed`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Friendly Dryad Action (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`She shows you a magical place in the forest (see e198 and ignore the * after the event, although you may not hunt today)`],[`3–4`,`She instructs the spirits of the forest to guide you — -1 to all Lost rolls until you leave a forest hex. Although you may not hunt, woodland creatures provide foraged food for you each day until you leave a forest hex`],[`5–6`,`She gives you a magical gift — see e195`]]}]},{tag:`e179`,title:`Tournament`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`The Local Lord is holding a tournament today. Purchased food is free (r215) but lodging (r217) here is twice the cost due to the crowds. No other normal actions are available today but you may roll twice if you Seek to Hire (r210) tomorrow. May just watch the contests or enter one by paying 5 gold. Roll 1D6 for type of contest.`,note:`If you or a character in your party wins a contest: you gain 15 gold and roll 1D6 — 1→you meet the local lord (e130); 2–6→add +1 to your 2D6 roll if you Seek an Audience (r211) tomorrow.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Contest Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`Sword Battle — duel a knight (S6, E6 — see r305). Contest ends when either combatant is knocked unconscious. If you win: roll 1D6 — on 6 the bested knight becomes your ally (r334)`],[`4–5`,`Archery — roll 2D6 adding ranged Skill (r220i) of participating character. You win on 16+; on 20+ you split your opponent's arrow and win 25 gold instead`],[`6`,`Arena Contest — see e072`]]}]},{tag:`e180`,title:`Healing Potion`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This bright and clear potion can be applied once to any character (including yourself) at the end of the day after the evening meal (r215). The potion immediately cures all wounds except poison wounds.`},{tag:`e181`,title:`Cure Poison Vial`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`Any character can drink this vial once during the evening meal (r215). It will cure all poison wounds overnight. Only poison wounds are cured — it has no effect on regular wounds.`},{tag:`e182`,title:`Gift of Charm`,type:`item`,image:!0,desc:`This small item is of no real value but has a magic aura. You can give the gift to any character(s) you encounter as part of any talk or negotiate option. You can then roll a second and a third time for that option selecting whichever result you prefer.`,note:`Once given away the gift is gone unless the receiver fights you and you kill him — then you can recover the gift as part of his possessions.`},{tag:`e183`,title:`Endurance Sash`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`You wear this sash around your waist and its magic adds +1 to your normal Endurance level. The effect is permanent as long as you retain your possessions.`,note:`You cannot wear more than one sash — additional ones can be cached (r214) or given to other characters in your party.`},{tag:`e184`,title:`Resistance Talisman`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This jewelled talisman allows you to resist all magic spells and attacks. Whenever magic is used you can call upon the talisman to negate it. However the talisman may be unable to contain a strong spell — each time it is used roll 1D6: on 6 the spell is stopped but the talisman is shattered and broken.`},{tag:`e185`,title:`Poison Drug`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This drug can be applied to the weapons of any one character in your party. Its use means that whenever that character strikes in combat (r220) and inflicts wounds: for each normal wound given one extra poison wound is also given.`,note:`After a combat where the poisoned weapon is used: roll 1D6 — on 6 the poison has worn off and the weapon returns to normal. Since a character often has multiple weapons he has the option of using his poisoned weapon or a normal weapon as desired.`},{tag:`e186`,title:`Magic Sword`,type:`item`,image:!0,desc:`A character can carry this special sword among his weapons and adds +1 to the combat skill of the character with it. In addition the blade's magic means that every wound it inflicts counts as poisoned.`,note:`When you first discover this item: roll 1D6 — on 6 see e186a. e186a The Dawnsword (Ψ): You realise that this sword once belonged to your father and is shrouded in legend, crafted by the Wizards of Light from ages past. The sword adds +2 to your Skill and adds +1 to your W&W when rolling in combat references (r301–r309).`},{tag:`e187`,title:`Anti-Poison Amulet`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This protects against all poison wounds. Any poison wound inflicted is ignored if the target has this amulet. Normal wounds still take effect.`,note:`When a poison wound is prevented: roll 2D6 — if the total is 12 the amulet has reached its limit, cannot absorb more poison and must be discarded.`},{tag:`e188`,title:`Pegasus Mount`,type:`item`,image:!0,desc:`You have acquired a Pegasus, a winged horse that allows you to travel airborne. The pegasus is like a normal mount in all other respects including the same transport ability (r206), food requirements (r215) and lodging when in towns/castles/temples (r217). You can also use the pegasus as a normal mount on the ground if desired.`,note:`If not stabled (r217) in a habitation overnight: a Pegasus will be stolen on a roll of 2+.`},{tag:`e189`,title:`Charisma Talisman`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`The character that wears this talisman improves his stature and charisma in the eyes of others. If you wear it: add +1 to your W&W in any event or option that involves or results from talk or negotiation. This talisman does not improve your W&W when trying to evade, hide, attack or surprise.`,note:`Like many magical devices this talisman's spell may eventually wear out. After each use: roll 2D6 — if the total is 12 it has worn out and must be discarded.`},{tag:`e190`,title:`Death Mist Potion`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This sealed jar is filled with a deadly and quick-acting gas created by a master alchemist. If you surprise an enemy in combat (r220): instead of your initial strikes your entire party can stand off and let you hurl the potion. When you do: roll 1D6 for each character encountered.`,note:`The jar with the gas is rather heavy and counts as one (1) load to transport (r206).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Death Mist Effect (1D6 per character)`,rows:[[`1–4`,`Character killed by gas`],[`5`,`Character flees from gas taking their wealth and possessions with them`],[`6`,`Character unaffected by gas`]]}]},{tag:`e191`,title:`Resistance Ring`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This ring creates a magic aura around the wearer. Every time the wearer is wounded: roll 2D6 for effect. The ring can also be used to ward poison wounds like normal wounds and to ward magic attacks against the wearer (but will not protect others in the party). Against a magic attack: two rolls must be made and the best result (to the wearer) is applied.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Ring Effect (2D6)`,rows:[[`2–8`,`The blow is warded away by the ring — ignore all wounds`],[`9–11`,`The blow skids around the aura and strikes home — take normal wound result minus one wound which was deflected`],[`12`,`The ring fails and the blow has normal effect. The ring melts on your finger causing injury and one extra wound`]]}]},{tag:`e192`,title:`Resurrection Necklace`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This necklace of black opals and tiny bones holds the secret of a second life. If the wearer dies for any reason: at the end of that day the character rises from the dead. The necklace disintegrates as the character revives and thus only works once. The resurrection occurs in the same hex and the character is free to select any action on the next day.`,note:`A character revived by the necklace having been left for dead will have lost all possessions and money and the entire party will have scattered (although a lover might return, r228). A character revived by the necklace has a somewhat ghoulish cast and is a bit weaker — their Endurance is reduced by one. Such appearances are favoured at Drogat Castle and so you can add +1 if Seeking an Audience (r211) with Count Drogat.`},{tag:`e193`,title:`Shield of Light`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`This enchanted shield will flash and shine in the eyes of any attacker. When a character has this shield: any opponent in combat has their Skill reduced by -1. If the character with the shield dies: the shield will dull and die becoming useless.`,note:`At the end of each combat where the shield is used: roll 1D6 — on 6 the shield is so damaged by battle that it is now useless. To preserve the shield you may elect to not use it in some combats. You can change your mind during the battle but must then check for damage after the battle anyway.`},{tag:`e194`,title:`Royal Helm of the Northlands`,type:`item`,image:!0,desc:`(Ψ) This ancient and sacred treasure has long been lost. Myths and tales still relate the great exploits of its wearers. You hold the treasure that gives you automatic and indisputable right to the Northlands throne.`,note:`If you return to either Ogon (0101) or Weshor (1501) with the helm: you will be instantly hailed as the rightful King of the Northlands and win the game. In the meantime the helm increases your stature and self-confidence — increase your W&W by +1.`},{tag:`e195`,title:`Magical Possession`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`You have found or been given a magical item. Roll 2D6 for the specific item.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Magical Item (2D6)`,rows:[[`2`,`e191 – Resistance Ring`],[`3`,`e186 – Magic Sword`],[`4`,`e189 – Charisma Talisman`],[`5`,`e184 – Resistance Talisman`],[`6`,`e187 – Anti-Poison Amulet`],[`7`,`e180 – Healing Potion`],[`8`,`e182 – Gift of Charm`],[`9`,`e193 – Shield of Light`],[`10`,`e183 – Endurance Sash`],[`11`,`e189 – Charisma Talisman`],[`12`,`e192 – Resurrection Necklace`]]}]},{tag:`e196`,title:`Winged Boots`,type:`item`,image:!1,desc:`You have found a pair of magical boots that will allow the wearer to fly. You may use Airborne Travel (r204d) and can use escape flying (r313) rather than rolling on an evade option in an encounter (abandoning party members that cannot fly).`,note:`If in combat: the wearer (only) may add +1 to any escape (r220e) attempt.`},{tag:`e197`,title:`Animal Trainer`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You have found a clever trainer of animals who may have some to sell to you. Roll 1D6 for the animals on sale today (rolling twice if at a castle).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Animals for Sale (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`Horses — 10 gold each. Enduring (not affected by e078) and will not be affected by starvation of fodder for first day only`],[`3`,`A trained falcon (see e107) — 4 gold`],[`4`,`A loyal hunting dog — 5 gold. It is S3, E3 and must be fed each day but adds +1 to Hunting (r215b)`],[`5`,`A magical familiar like a cat or owl — 12 gold. If given to a magician/wizard/witch: the character gains +1 Endurance`],[`6`,`A flying Pegasus mount (see e188) — 50 gold`]]}]},{tag:`e198`,title:`Forest Magic`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`As you travel through the forest the air becomes charged with ancient woodland magic. If you have an elf/magician/wizard/druid/witch in your party: may roll 1D6 below to see what you have found and then decide if you wish to continue or end the event. Without such a character: must decide to continue or not before rolling the die.`,note:`*After completing the event, roll 1D6. On a 5-6 (4-6 if at a woodland ruin), you encounter a magical creature – roll 1D6: 1: e080; 2-3: e178; 4-5: e198a; 6: e198b. Also, if you have a druid, witch or elf in your party, they advise you not to hunt or fish here (r215b), although characters may forage normally. If you do not have such a character in your party, or decide to hunt anyway, see e198c.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Forest Magic Discovery (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`Elf Temple: find the hidden temple of an elf forest cult — see e066. All priests/monks are elves. If elf in party: +1 to Seek Audience (r211). May only Make an Offering (r212) with an elf in your party`],[`2`,`Stone Circle: circle of ancient standing stones. If druid in party: tomorrow he may pray here to gain +1S or Make an Offering (r212) with +2 to the roll. Also roll 1D6 for possible event: 1–3→None; 4→Hostile Druid (e022, Fight option); 5→e019; 6→e045`],[`3`,`Faery Ring*: circle of large colourful mushrooms. May choose for any characters to eat a mushroom — roll 1D6 each: 1–3→they taste lovely, character does not need food tonight; 4→Poisoned, take 1 poisoned wound; 5→Magic, magician/wizard/witch/elf gains +1S, otherwise character heals 1 wound; 6→Healing, mushroom totally heals character of all normal and poisoned wounds`],[`4`,`Magical Pool*: calming pool beneath a small waterfall. May choose for any characters to drink or bathe — roll 1D6 each: 1–3→calm and refreshing but no other effect; 4→Healing, if drunk heal a wound, if bathed heal all wounds; 5→Poison, if drunk take a poison wound, if bathed killed; 6→Strength, if drunk gain +1S until tomorrow night, if bathed gain +1S permanently`],[`5`,`Enchanted Grove*: grove of huge twisting trees and beautiful flowers in golden sunlight. Any magicians/wizards/witches/elves gain +1E. Other characters can all heal 1 wound. Also roll 1D6 — on 6 you find a magical item, roll on Treasure Table B`],[`6`,`Woodland Ruin*: old ruin of a keep covered in leaves, branches, roots and brambles — see e064. If elf or druid in party: may roll twice on search ruins (r208) actions and select either event`]]}]},{tag:`e198a`,title:`Living Forest`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`Each member of your party is attacked (r306) by tree branches and roots that count as S3, E3*. The character cannot escape the combat unless the roots attacking them have been overcome. However, if you have either a witch or druid in your party, roll 1D6 and on a 4-6 they can cast a spell which calms the forest and the attack is cancelled.`},{tag:`e198b`,title:`Living Forest`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You encounter an old and mischievous spirit of the forest. If you have a witch, druid or elf in your party, they communicate with the spirit who will act as your guide and allow you to succeed in making any ‘Lost’ rolls (r205) until you leave a forest hex. If you do not have such a character, the wood spirit takes a dislike to intruders and makes forest paths disappear before you. Until you leave a forest hex, add +1 to all ‘Lost’ rolls (r205).`},{tag:`e198c`,title:`Hunting in the Magical Forest`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`As you hunt for your evening meal, a white stag appears from out of the forest in front of you. Roll 1D6 and if you roll greater than your Wit & Wiles, you fire an arrow at the stag. Roll 1D6 for each of your followers too (except a druid, elf, halfling, magician, wizard or witch) and if the roll is a 1, they fire an arrow too. If anyone fires an arrow at the white stag, a spirit of the forest attacks you – see e198a and you have +1 to any ‘Lost’ rolls (r205) until you leave a forest hex. If you do not fire an arrow at the stag then you automatically succeed in any ‘Lost’ rolls until you leave a forest hex. Either way, you do not gain any food units (215a) through the hunt tonight.`},{tag:`e199`,title:`Carnival`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`A colourful carnival is visiting the habitation. If you decide to visit: any food you buy is available at half price (round fractions up) and you need not pay any members of your party today due to the fun they have. If you decide not to visit: roll 1D6 for each other party member — on 5–6 they desert due to your mean spirit. Roll 1D6 for a further event.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Carnival Event (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`The carnival is part of a festival here — see e169`],[`2`,`A tournament is taking place here — see e179`],[`3–4`,`You meet someone at the carnival — roll 1D6: 1→e049; 2→e073; 3–4→e172; 5→e173; 6→e023`],[`5`,`A garish tent catches your eye. The carnival hawker promises delights and danger for 5 gold. If you pay and brave the experience (alone): roll 1D6 — 1→e046; 2→e047; 3–4→e172a; 5→e041; 6→Nothing but distorting mirrors`],[`6`,`You discover the carnival is a cover for creatures of the night. Roll 1D6 for your encounter: 1–2→e170; 3→e023 (Dark); 4→e146; 5→e073 (Hostile); 6→e032`]]}]},{tag:`e200`,title:`The Shade of King Hendryc`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`(Ψ) The ghostly apparition of an old King appears before you but with shock you realise that the shade is actually your poor dead father reaching out from beyond the grave. Although overcome with grief your heart is strengthened with his wisdom. Before fading back into the shadows King Hendryc enchants your faithful broadsword Bonebiter so that it is now magical (e186). He also shares some ancient knowledge — roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Ancient Knowledge (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`e147 – Clue to Treasure`],[`4–6`,`e176 – The Secret of the Ruins`]]}]},{tag:`e399`,title:`Ogab's Scroll`,type:`story`,image:!1,desc:`Sire, I hope I have served you well and you escaped across the border to the South... Roll 1D6 for what Ogab has left for you.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Scroll Contents (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`I have managed to hide some gold for you. If you go to hex 0303 and search successfully for a cache (r214): gain Wealth code 30`],[`2`,`I have enclosed a letter for the Mayor of Cumry — perhaps he can help? See e157 for hex 1004`],[`3`,`I hear the ruins of Jakor's Keep might hold valuable secrets. If you go there (hex 0901): may roll twice the first time you roll on r208 and select either result`],[`4`,`I bring good news — Darios, your loyal guard captain has escaped to the Temple of Zhor (hex 1805). If you go there: see e069`],[`5`,`I studied the Southlands for knowledge that may be useful to your quest — see e162`],[`6`,`But sadly these rations and coins are all I could store. Gain 3 food units and 2 gold`]]}]},{tag:`e400`,title:`Hooded Figure`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`(Ψ) You encounter a mysterious hooded figure watching you silently from the shadows. The face is shrouded in darkness yet you get a shiver up your spine. You must choose to either avoid the figure or dare to approach, then roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Avoid (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`The figure vanishes into the shadows — event ends`],[`3–6`,`The figure tries to block your escape — roll for their Identity and encounter them using their Evade option`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Approach (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`The figure vanishes into the shadows and is gone`],[`2`,`The figure beckons you to follow. If you do: complete a Follow action (r219) and then roll again here with +1 to roll. Or think better of it and roll on the Avoid choice above`],[`3`,`The figure tries to avoid your approach. If you roll less than or equal to your W&W on 1D6: you may encounter the figure (see result 4–6). Otherwise the figure vanishes from sight before you can catch them`],[`4–6`,`You encounter the figure — roll for their Identity and then encounter them choosing to either Talk or Fight`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Identity (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e023 (-1 to Dark roll)`],[`2`,`e073`],[`3`,`e172`],[`4`,`e170`],[`5`,`e425`],[`6`,`e200`]]}]},{tag:`e401`,title:`Escaped Prisoner`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`A prisoner has escaped from the town jail and is hiding in the forest (hex 0302). If you return to Ogon with the head of the prisoner: the mayor will reward you with 30 gold and grant you an audience (see e156). If you accept the bounty and travel to the hex: after all other events are concluded roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Search Result (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`You find tracks but the prisoner has moved one hex east`],[`2–3`,`You encounter the escaped prisoner S6, E4 (see r303)`],[`4–5`,`e048 – Fugitive`],[`6`,`(Ψ) The prisoner is your loyal servant Ogab (S4, E4, W2) who will join you as a staunch ally (r334) and will never voluntarily desert you irrespective of events or food and lodging requirements. Whilst Ogab is with you: add +1 to your W&W for his support and advice`]]}]},{tag:`e402`,title:`Bandit Threat`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You discover that the village is under constant threat from northern bandits who demand gold or attack the farms and villagers. The next attack is due in 1D6 days. If you agree to help Weshor: you gain free food and lodging and each day you may make a W&W roll — if you roll less than or equal to your score you may roll on the Seek to Hire Followers table (r210) for villagers who will join you in the fight at no cost. When the attack comes, you may not take any daily actions but must encounter the bandits. Roll 1D6: 1-3: See e051 for your attackers and either pay them 50 gold or see r305; 4-6: See e002 and roll on Fight option.`,note:`If you defeat the bandits, the Weshor villagers that joined you in the fight will become allies and join your party at no cost. The grateful village elder also grants an audience – see e177 with +2 to the roll.`},{tag:`e403`,title:`Danger from the Sky`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You discover that the town of Cumry is being terrorised by a cruel dragon that attacks from above destroying buildings with its fiery breath! Roll 1D6 after any daily actions (r203) in this hex — on 1 the dragon attacks! See e098 (choose to evade or fight with no lair roll). If you choose to fight: you will be joined by 1D6+1 townsfolk (each S4, E3) desperately trying to defend Cumry. The dragon attacks for 1D6 rounds before swooping back off into the night sky. May also try to track the dragon to its lair — roll for a random direction then 1D3 for distance in hexes. When you reach the destination hex after resolving any normal travel events (r207): search for the lair — roll 1D6: 1–4→Find the lair (see e098, automatic lair option); 5–6→Find clues but not the lair — may search again tomorrow but roll 1D6 and if the result is 1 the dragon finds you (see e098 but with no lair option and +1 to encounter option rolls)!`,note:`If you kill the dragon: the townsfolk of Cumry will reward you with free food (r215) and lodging (r217) for you and your party forever and the town mayor will grant you an audience (see e156, result 5).`},{tag:`e404`,title:`A Halfling Quest`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The Halflings would like you to help with a problem in the village.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Quest Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`e404a`],[`4–6`,`e404b`]]}]},{tag:`e404a`,title:`Missing Children`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You learn that several halfling children went ‘adventuring’ in the surrounding area two days ago and have not returned! If you don’t agree to help find them, the halflings will ‘dislike you’ (see e070). If you do agree to help, roll for a random direction in which the children set off. If you reach this hex, you may search as a daily action (r203) and roll 1D6 (add +1 to roll in a Farmland or Countryside hex):`,note:`If you return the children to the halfling village, there is a celebration where your party gains free food and lodging for a week plus an audience with the halfling mayor (see e070a with +2 to the roll).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Quest Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`You find nothing, but can search again tomorrow;`],[`4–5`,`You find tracks, but they have moved into a new random hex, so you must try again in that hex;`],[`6+`,`You find the rather scared halfling children (count as combined S2, E3). Add +2 to the chance of having a travel event today (r204b) as the children have a habit of finding trouble...`]]}]},{tag:`e404b`,title:`Haunted Mill`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You learn that there is an old ruined mill on the edge of the halfling village. The halflings avoid the place and speak of strange noises coming from it at night. Halfling gossip suggests it is haunted. If you investigate the mill as a daily action (r203), roll 1D6:`,note:`If you investigate the mill and kill any inhabitants, the halflings give you free food (r215) and lodging (r217) for tonight and tomorrow, together with 30 gold.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Quest Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e075`],[`2`,`e073`],[`3`,`e137`],[`4`,`e032`],[`5`,`e052`],[`6`,`It’s just rumour and crows!`]]}]},{tag:`e405`,title:`Stolen Relic`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You discover that the Holy Staff of Zhor, an artifact that cures the sick, has been stolen from the temple by raiding orcs last night and the priests ask for your help. If you refuse, you must leave this hex and never return. Any monks or priests in your party will immediately leave you in anger too. The orcs currently have a lead of less than a day, so if you set off immediately, before your evening meal (r215), you might catch them. Roll randomly for the direction the orcs took. If your party is mounted, you catch them and must see e055. If not, roll 2D6 and if you roll at least 2 under the chance of being Lost in this terrain (r205), you catch the orcs (e055). If not, they have eluded you and travelled to another random hex. When you go there, you can try to catch them again.`,note:`If you kill the orcs, you can recover the Holy Staff. If this is returned to the Temple of Zhor, you may choose to Make an Offering (r212) with +3 to the roll, or have an Audience with the High Priest (e155 with +2 to roll). Alternatively, you can decide to selfishly keep the Staff. If you do, the Staff functions as a Healing Potion (e180) but also cures poison wounds (r222a). Roll 1D6 for the number of times you may use the Staff. If you keep the Staff, it is like you have an ‘unholy mark’ (see e018).`},{tag:`e406`,title:`Deadly Plague`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You learn that the townsfolk of Anglae are dying from a terrifying and deadly plague of unknown origin. You may also have been infected! Roll 1D6 for each character in your party — on 1 see r227 (result 4). To help the town and cure the plague (including your characters if any are affected), you must journey to the temple of Branwyn (hex 0711) for healing herbs and ointments which will be freely given by the priestesses. Once you reach the temple, any infected characters in your party are cured instantly. Alternatively, if you have a witch or druid in your party, they may forage for healing herbs in any forest hex as a daily action (r203). Roll 1D6-3 for the number of herbs they collect each day. You will need 10 in total.`,note:`Whichever method is chosen, you must return to Anglae within the next twelve days or it will be too late. If you leave a priest or monk in Anglae to help tend the sick however, each such character adds +2 to the number of days you have. If you cure the plague, the Mayor of Anglae gives you a wealth code 100 as a reward. If you fail to cure the plague, you cannot enter Anglae for the rest of the game. If you decided not to help the town, you become cursed (see e018 with an ‘unholy mark’).`},{tag:`e407`,title:`Failed Crops`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You learn that the crops around Cawther have all failed this season. You must pay triple for any food that you buy here (r217). You may also gain double the price of any food that you sell here at market (r231). If you have a magician, wizard, witch or priest in your party then you realize that the crop failure is the result of dark magic and that Cawther has been cursed. To lift the curse requires a fairly simple incantation but only with the purest water from a mountain spring.`,note:`If you go to a mountain hex, you may search for a spring as a daily action (r203) and roll 1D6: 1-2: You cannot find a spring in this hex and must search elsewhere; 3-4: You find nothing today but can continue your search tomorrow here; 5-6: You find a spring and can fill your waterskin with the water (counts as 1 load – r206). If you return to Cawther with the water, you can lift the curse and the villagers will reward youwith a purse of wealth code 50, plus a Letter of Recommendation (e157) to the Baron of Huldra Castle.`},{tag:`e408`,title:`Quest for the Dwarf Lord`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You discover that the Dwarf Lord of the Mine could use your help. Roll 1D6: on 1–3 see e408a; on 4–6 see e408b.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Quest Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`e408a Awakened Danger: The dwarves have dug too deep and something that should have been left undisturbed has awoken — miners have been slaughtered. If you volunteer: the dwarves assign a dwarf miner to your party (S5, E6, W10). May explore the mines as a daily action (r203) — must leave mounts in stables. Roll 2D6 (+1 per dwarf in party, +1 per previous time you rolled here): 2–5→Lost in tunnels, must roll 9+ on 2D6 to find bearings next turn (modifiers as above); 6→narrow ledges in huge caverns — roll 2D6 for each character, on 12 they fall to their death; 7–9→venture deeper but find nothing today; 10+→discover the awakened evil — roll 1D6: 1–2→e098 (automatic lair); 3–4→e428; 5→e032 (+3 to number encountered); 6→e057 (but encounter two). If not 10+: eat evening meal in mines (r215) and may leave or search again tomorrow. If you kill what lies in the mines: the dwarves will give you gems (e142) as a reward`],[`4–6`,`e408b The Lost Hammer of Falin: The dwarves have found the location of old tombs where Falin, one of the first Dwarf smiths, is said to have been buried alongside his mythical Hammer. If you agree: the Dwarf Lord assigns a dwarf warrior (S6, E6, W10) to your party (r334). If you travel to the tombs (hex 1902): may search like a cache (r214) and if you discover them [Site] roll 1D6 (twice and choose either result if a dwarf is with you): 1→tomb is empty, all dwarves desert in sorrow; 2→Ghosts of 1D6+1 dwarf warriors (each S4, E3*, W0) rise to defend the tomb (r308); 3→Goblins have raided the tomb — roll 2D6 for number (each S3, E3, W1) see r306; 4→tomb guarded by traps — roll twice on r229 applied to a random character each time (r343); 5→spectre of Falin appears (e082, ignore e200) unless dwarf in party; 6→tomb is unguarded and the fabled Hammer of Falin is within your grasp. If you recover the Hammer and return it to the Mines: the Lord raises a dwarf army to help you regain your throne and you win the game! The Hammer is a magical weapon granting +1S to any who wield it (+2S if a dwarf wields it and +1S to all other dwarves in your party)`]]}]},{tag:`e409`,title:`Attack of the Dead`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Postponed until the end of the day. You are suddenly woken from your sleep by the cries of the monks. The temple of Donat is being attacked by undead warriors! Scrambling for your weapons your party must face 1D6+1 zombies (each S5, E5*, W0) — see r308.`,note:`If you survive the attack: you discover that the temple has been desecrated by the undead warriors. You cannot Make an Offering (r212) here for the rest of the game. The shaken monks tell you of a desert ruins that must have spawned the evil. If you go to hex 1407 and successfully search (see r214): see e064.`},{tag:`e410`,title:`Abducted High Priestess`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You discover that Branwyn, the high priestess of this temple, has been kidnapped by mercenaries. Until she is returned to the temple you may not Seek an Audience here (r211). The priestesses are forbidden to pay the 150 gold ransom for their high priestess but you may instead when the mercenaries return in 3 days. If you leave the temple without helping: you are given an unholy mark (see e018).`,note:`May alternatively track the mercenaries to their camp and rescue Branwyn. To find the location of the camp: roll 1D6 — if less than W&W roll 1D6 for a random direction then 1D3 for the distance in hexes. If you have a magician/wizard/witch/elf/druid in your party: their magic will also be able to find the camp. When you reach the camp: must attack (r303) and defeat the mercenaries — leader (†S6, E6, W50) and 2D6 troops (minimum 5, each S5, E4, W4). If Branwyn is returned to the temple: roll 1D6 (+1 if she was rescued): 1–2→your party is fed and lodged (r217) here for free until you leave the temple; 3→the priestesses heal the wounds of every character in your party; 4→Branwyn helps you ask the Gods for guidance — add +2 to Make an Offering (r212) tomorrow; 5→you are shown to the holy sanctum of the temple (see e044, adding +3 to the roll); 6+→you fall in love with her (r228) and escape (r218) the temple. She is S2, E3 and W100 in temple treasures she has stolen! You can never return to the temple however.`},{tag:`e411`,title:`Intrigue at Huldra Castle`,type:`story`,image:!1,desc:`You have learnt that all is not as it seems at Huldra Castle. Roll 1D6: on 1–2 see e144; on 3–6 see e411a.`,note:`e411a Dark Advisor: Baron Huldra has a very strange and sinister seneschal named Kharak. This pale advisor seems to have incredible influence over the Baron and is feared throughout the entire castle. Subtract -1 from your roll if trying to Seek an Audience with the Baron (r211) and -1 to the Audience roll (e152). On a result of e148 Kharak is immune to bribes and you are instead arrested (e060). Each night you stay at the castle: roll 1D6 for each wizard/magician/witch in your party — on 1–2 the character has been abducted by unknown forces. If you wish to investigate Kharak: counts as a Seek News daily action (r209) with any rumour result indicating you gain 1 clue. If you gain 3 clues or roll a secret informant result (with any result except bogus secrets): see e412b. After any Seek News (r209) action: roll 1D6 — if greater than W&W you attract attention of the castle guards (e050 as if you are wanted). May instead try to break into Kharak's rooms (r224b) — if successful see e412b. If discovered: see e024 (as if a Dark Wizard). e412b Cult of Dark Wizards (Ψ): You discover that Kharak is a Dark Wizard and a member of the cult that controls the usurpers to your Northlands throne! Must either escape Huldra Castle (r218) or confront Kharak (see e024 with +1 to the roll for each wizard/magician/witch that has left your party or vanished here). If you kill Kharak: gain an additional Wealth of 110 and have an immediate Audience with Baron Huldra (e152) with +1 to the roll.`},{tag:`e412`,title:`A Quest for the Woodland Realm`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`The Elf King requires your help in an urgent matter. Roll 1D6: on 1–4 see e412a; on 5–6 see e412b.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Quest Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–4`,`e412a Bring Light to the Wood: Evil had begun venturing closer to the realm of the Elf King and must be stopped. If you slay the creatures you will be rewarded with an Audience (e166a, adding +2 to roll). The King lends you his female elf guard captain (S6, E5, W7) to help. Choose a forest hex 3 hexes from the Haven. When you reach there: roll 1D6 for the creatures you encounter (do not roll for a normal Travel event in that hex): 1→e098; 2→e073; 3→e074; 4→e055; 5→e033; 6→e057`],[`5–6`,`e412b The Ashes of Ilmari: Ulrak, a wizard from your Northern homelands has arrived at the Elf Haven carrying the ashes of a powerful Sorceress of Light killed by the fiery breath of a dragon. He seeks advice from the Elf King about magic to bring Ilmari back to life to lead the desperate fight against the Dark Wizards. Roll 1D6 for the number of days that pass whilst Ulrak and the Elf King consult ancient magical texts. There are legends of a magical spring deep in the Samain Marshes (hex 1526) which could resurrect Ilmari. If you wish to take this quest: Ulrak will accompany you (see e023 for statistics and abilities) and the Elf King will also send his son to help giving your party elven cloaks (see e166a, result 5) and mounts too. If you reach the Marshes [Site]: see e198 and if you survive the encounter you may then bathe Ilmari's ashes in the spring. There is a blinding flash of light and the sorceress is returned to life. Ilmari is deeply moved and grateful to you so uses her incredible magic powers to transport you back to the Northlands where she destroys the usurpers to your throne. You have won the game!`]]}]},{tag:`e413`,title:`A Thief in the Night`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`If you have other characters in your party: see instead e173. If you are alone: whilst walking through the streets of Tulith you see a gang of four thieves attacking another man. You may walk the other way and ignore the assault or intervene. If you decide to help: each thief is S4, E4, W15 — see r301.`,note:`If you defeat the thieves: the man thanks you and introduces himself as Garon (S4, E4, W30) — another thief! Over food and beer at the local tavern (you need not eat further today as Garon pays) he offers you a job as his personal bodyguard. The pay will be 10 gold per day. If you accept: must stay in Tulith for at least another week and Garon insists on a contract of at least 3 jobs. At the start of each day: roll 1D6 — 1→more thieves find the two of you (see e173); 2–4→free to carry out whatever daily action (r203) you wish as Garon is busy; 5–6→Garon wants you to accompany him on a job. You both spend the day casing the joint before robbing a house (r224a, +1 to W&W roll due to Garon's experience). After either a week or 3 robberies (whichever is longest): you can decide to continue with Garon or he will reluctantly honour the contract and let you leave. Before you do so however: he has one last job the two of you could do — robbing the thieves' guild of Tulith! If you accept: see e173 (final paragraph).`},{tag:`e414`,title:`The Laughing Wolf`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You have found The Laughing Wolf, the best tavern in Erwyn, run by the jovial Mikal and his adopted daughter Tila. The food is good, drinks are plentiful and there always seems to be something happening in the tavern.`,note:`If you Visit the Tavern (r230): may roll twice and choose either result. If you meet someone here (result 10): may also roll twice and choose. If you visit at least three times: you become a regular so need not pay the 1 gold for drinks and may purchase food (r215) at half price. Also roll 1D6 each day you subsequently visit here as Tila takes a bit of a shine to you — on 6 you fall in love (r228) and she joins you on your adventures. Tila is S3, E3, W10 and brings 2 horses.`},{tag:`e415`,title:`Haunting of the Moor`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`You hear about an old legend that tells of a maiden from Galden that fell in love with a wandering mage. They fled the village pursued by a mob and rode north to the swamps and mists of Llewyla Moor where they were said to meet a grisly fate and now their spirits haunt there unable to find rest. The legend also speaks of a treasure that the woman stole to be with her lover.`,note:`If you wish to investigate the legend: go to hex 0213 [Site] and roll 1D6: 1–2→you search thoroughly but find nothing — it was just a legend; 3–4→as darkness falls eerie lights dance across the swamp and two wraiths appear (each †S6 / S5 with priest, E3*, can only be hurt by magical weapons or wounds from a wizard/magician) and attack (r308) — if you kill the ghostly wraiths roll 1D6 for wealth (r225) of the treasure: 1→0; 2→12; 3–4→25; 5→60; 6→110; 5–6→the ghostly couple appear before you more sad than threatening. They lead you silently to the remains of their mortal bodies and you realise they wish to be buried properly. If you agree to take their remains to Galden, a chapel (e174) or any temple: the woman points to a bejewelled necklace lying in the swamp (worth 100 gold in any town or castle market) — it is yours. If you take the necklace without making the bargain: the wraiths attack you (see result 3–4 for stats, r307).`},{tag:`e416`,title:`Danger in the Sewers`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You discover that Halowich has an extensive system of sewers that twist underneath the town to help provide drainage for the surrounding farmlands. But there are rumours of things living down there too. If you want to explore the sewers of Halowich as a daily action (r203): roll 2D6.`,note:`e416a Flooding Tunnel: the sewer waters rise — you have to escape! Roll 1D6 for each character — on 1 they are drowned with all possessions. e416b Lost in the Dark: you become lost in the sewers. Cannot eat any food (r215) down here and must starve (r216) due to the filthy conditions (although followers cannot desert you, r216a). Roll 1D6 for each character — on 1 they suffer a wound. Tomorrow: roll 2D6 to try and leave the sewers — if you roll 8+ you are still lost and must spend another day here (roll 2D6 above for any encounter); any other result means you escape the sewers of Halowich. e416c Dead Body: you find a body covered in rat bites floating in the waters. Roll 1D6 for wealth code: 1→0; 2→4; 3→10; 4→25; 5→50; 6→60. e416d Thieves Guild: a tunnel leads to a secret entrance to the thieves guild. You see 2 thieves (each S4, E4, W7) guarding a treasure chest. May either volunteer to join the guild (see r224) or attack them to try and steal the treasure (r302). Roll 1D6 at the end of each round after the second — on 1–2 an additional 1D3 thieves (same stats) arrive to defend the guild. If you manage to kill the thieves: roll 1D6 — 1–2→W70; 3–4→e039; 5–6→e040.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Sewer Exploration (2D6)`,rows:[[`2`,`e097 – Marsh Gas and Rot`],[`3`,`e094 – Crocodiles`],[`4`,`e416a – Flooding Tunnel`],[`5`,`e432 – Rats`],[`6`,`e416b – Lost in the Dark`],[`7`,`The sewers are dank but you discover nothing`],[`8`,`e048 – Fugitive`],[`9`,`e416c – Dead Body`],[`10`,`e416d – Thieves Guild`],[`11`,`e046 – Gateway to Darkness`],[`12`,`e427 – Lair of Lizard-men`]]}]},{tag:`e417`,title:`The Plains of Datha`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`While you are in Brigud you hear the villagers talking about Datha in hushed frightened voices. If you buy them drinks for 3 gold or if you roll 1D6 and roll less than your W&W: you discover who or what Datha is — roll 1D6 below. Datha can be found in hex 0617 [Site]. You can also travel north to find out for yourself and then roll 1D6 below for what you find there.`,note:`If you return to Brigud with your tale: you will be given free food (r215) and lodging (r217) that night plus free drinks if you visit the tavern (r230).`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`What is Datha? (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e118a – Giant's Castle`],[`2`,`e068a – Wizard Tower`],[`3`,`e064 – Ruins`],[`4`,`e098 – Dragon`],[`5`,`e054 – Goblin Keep`],[`6`,`e056 – Orc Tower`]]}]},{tag:`e418`,title:`A Wizard's Fury`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`When exploring the tower as much as you dare you learn something. If you are alone: see e418a. If there are other characters in your party: see e418b.`,note:`e418a Imprisoned Fire Imp: a plaintive cry comes from behind a secure wooden door. You find yourself in an incredible magical laboratory with a fire imp trapped in a cage begging you to free her. She is bound in iron which stops her magic. Fire imps can be treacherous — can you trust her? If you release the imp: roll 1D6 — 1→the imp gives an evil laugh and sends a fireball in your direction (take 1D6+1 wounds) then vanishes; 2→she bows to you blowing you a kiss before vanishing in a swirl of smoke; 3→the fire imp thanks you and shows you a magical treasure (e195) in the laboratory before she vanishes; 4→the fire imp thanks you and will transport you to any hex on the map within 3 hexes of the tower then kisses you before vanishing; 5–6→the fire imp is grateful and joins you as an ally (r334). She is S3, E3 with fireball magic (e023a, successful on 4–6) and has the knowledge of a wizard. She also has the power of teleportation — she and you (no other characters) can either escape a combat automatically (r220e) or travel up to 3 hexes in any direction. Each time she uses her ability the fire imp must take a wound. The imp also reveals a magical item in the laboratory (e195). Must leave the tower tomorrow before the wizard discovers the fire imp has been released. If you decide not to release the fire imp: she curses you but is helpless. May find a magical item if you roll less than W&W on 1D6 (e195). If you steal the item: must immediately escape (r218a). e418b Stolen Spell Book: a thief has burgled the tower and made off with a tome of arcane lore. If you refuse to help: the wizard gets angry and locks you in a dungeon (e062). If you agree: the wizard casts a spell on you — for every 3 days you do not return the book you will suffer a wound at end of that day! He then uses his magic to teleport you up to 3 hexes in the direction of the thief. When you reach the habitation hex: must roll less than W&W as a daily action (r203) to locate the thief (S4, E4, W15) — then either attack to retrieve the book (r301) or pay him 100 gold for it. If you return with the spell book: the wizard will reward you with wealth 110. Alternatively you can use the tome yourself — this negates the wizard's spell cast on you and gives you knowledge in any events as if a wizard was in your party. You cannot cast any magic spells however.`},{tag:`e419`,title:`Slavers on the Nesser River`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`While walking the streets of Lullwyn you see a townsman comforting his crying wife. Their son has been taken by a cruel gang of slavers that sell their captives along the Nesser River. You may ignore their plight (but must roll 1D6 for each of your followers — on 1–2 they desert you for being such a callous leader) or you may try to rescue the boy and the other slaves.`,note:`If you go to hex 1521: see e163, although there are 6 guards and the leader has W100. If you kill the slavers: you can release the slaves (roll to see if a released slave girl falls in love with you, see e163) and rescue the boy from Lullwyn. If you return the lad to the town: his parents reward you with 20 gold, a horse and free food (r215) and lodging (r217) tonight. In the morning: roll 1D6 — on 5–6 the son joins you for the adventurous life. He is S3, E4, W2 and can act as a guide within a hex of Lullwyn.`},{tag:`e420`,title:`Destiny in the Stars`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`(Ψ) Postponed until after your evening meal. Your night at Sulwyth Temple is disturbed by strange dreams and portents. Stars align in the constellations and in them you see your father King Hendryc reaching towards you. You do not heal any wounds (r222) last night due to your restlessness.`,note:`If you have any priests or monks in your party or if you successfully Seek an Audience with the High Priest of Sulwyth (result 2–6, but with no other result): then they can interpret the signs in the stars. You must purchase a star-glass from the observatory at Sulwyth Temple for 10 gold and travel to Mount Eira, the highest mountain in the Barrier Peaks (hex 0503) in exactly 14 days time. Once there: your destiny as the true ruler of the North will be revealed to you (2Ψ) — gain +2 to your W&W.`},{tag:`e421`,title:`The Druids of Duffyd Temple`,type:`location`,image:!1,desc:`The druids that gather at Duffyd Temple are masters at developing herbs and potions. Roll 1D6 for what they may sell you. He will also buy any of the above potions for half their listed price.`,note:`Duffyd Temple also has a ring of mysterious standing stones. A druid in your party may as a daily action (r203) tomorrow either pray here to gain +1S or Make an Offering (r212) action with +2 to the roll.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Druid Stock (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`A healing potion (e180) for 3 gold`],[`2`,`A cure poison vial (e181) for 5 gold`],[`3`,`Foulbane, a plant feared by some undead creatures for 1 gold`],[`4`,`Mushrooms that count as 3 food units but do not count as a load for 2 gold`],[`5`,`Fine herbs that add +1 when you Make an Offering (r212) for 5 gold`],[`6`,`Chaga drug (see e143) for 2 gold (Chaga has no use in the Temple of Duffyd however)`]]}]},{tag:`e422`,title:`Night Terrors`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You learn that Lower Drogat is plagued with creatures that prey on the living after dark. Villagers are abducted either never to be seen again or discovered with their bodies completely drained of blood.`,note:`Each night you remain in the village: unless you have any foulbane plant with you or have a priest in your party roll 1D6 — 1–2→the night passes uneventfully; 3–4→a random character in your party (r343 but female characters will always be taken first) is taken in the night — if you are chosen or are alone see e170; 5–6→see e170.`},{tag:`e423`,title:`Intrigue at Aeravir Castle`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`All is not as it seems at Aeravir Castle. Roll 1D6: on 1–2 see e145; on 3–6 see e423a.`,note:`e423a Cult of the Lady: After staying the night at the castle you awake to find Lady Aeravir standing over you pouring away the last remnants of the drugged wine that you drank. The Lady has taken all your gold and possessions and the rest of your party has been imprisoned in the castle dungeons. She tells you that you'll only have your wealth returned and your party released if you obey the Lady's commands. Some years ago Lady Aeravir bore an illegitimate child whom she abandoned due to her position as the high priestess of a cult that requires chastity. Now the Lady wants you to go to Lullwyn where the daughter lives with adopted parents and bring the child back to her at the castle. If you go to Lullwyn: you can try to find the daughter as a daily action (r203) by asking around the town and rolling 1D6 with a result less than or equal to your W&W. If you succeed: the child is rather overwhelmed by her heritage and sad to leave her life here but agrees to accompany you (S2, E2, W2). If you do not return the daughter back to Lady Aeravir: the characters who were imprisoned there are lost to you (although a true love may return, r228). Of course if you were alone it does not matter but your wealth and possessions are lost too. If you do find and return Aeravir's daughter: the Lady is not ungrateful and rewards you with 1D3×100 gold.`},{tag:`e424`,title:`Danger in the Giant's Castle`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`While staying at the huge castle of giants you discover something. Roll 1D6: on 1–3 see e424a; on 4–6 see e424b.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Discovery Type (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–3`,`e424a Imprisoned Princess: A feisty princess from the Eastern Isles is being held captive in the dungeons. If you could rescue her the reward could be more than you can possibly imagine. You can try to sneak into the dungeon and release her by making two 1D6 rolls where the result must be less than your W&W. If you fail either or both rolls: you are attacked (r307) by a giant on guard (S9, E8, W10). At the end of each round: roll 1D6 — on 1–2 another giant (identical stats) joins the combat. Alternatively: try to attack the giant with possibly surprise on your side (r301) rolling for more giant guards at the end of the first normal round. If you rescue the princess (S3, E3, W0): you escape (r218) the giant's castle. If you return the princess to any castle marked on the map: the Lord there will send messages to her father in the Eastern Isles who will help you retake your throne and you win the game. At the end of each full week while you are with the princess: roll 1D6 — on 6 you fall in love with her (r228)`],[`4–6`,`e424b The Giant's Crown: There is a chance to steal the giant Chieftain's magic Crown that binds the other giants to servitude. Roll 2D6 and add your W&W but subtract the number of characters in your party (including yourself): 7 or less→you are captured in the attempt and sentenced to death (e061); 8–10→you are discovered (r308) by the giant Chieftain (†S10, E9, W60) and a bodyguard (S9, E8, W10) — if you kill them you take the crown (see 11+ below); 11+→you steal the Crown to become the Chieftain of Giants and all the giants in the castle and the South are bound to you. Leading an army of giants north to retake your throne you win the game!`]]}]},{tag:`e425`,title:`Disguised Noble`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You have encountered a noble person who is travelling incognito. You may pass by and end the encounter or try to persuade them to join your party (r336). If they do join you: they require their own room if lodging (r217) and you will become a wanted man in their home hex.`,note:`If you return them home: may Seek an Audience with the Lord there (r211) with +3 to the roll. At the end of each full week you are with them: roll 1D6 — on 6 you fall in love (r228). Roll 1D6 for their identity: 1–2→Lyra, daughter of Baron Huldra (hex 1212) S3, E3, W30; 3–4→Eliane, priestess of Branwyn temple (hex 0711) S2, E4, W15; 5–6→Dirael, daughter of the Elf King (e166 at hex 1518) S4, E4, W12.`},{tag:`e426`,title:`The Darkness of Drogat Castle`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`You have learnt that all is not as it seems at Drogat Castle. Roll 1D6: on 1–2 see e146; on 3–6 see e426a.`,note:`e426a Castle of Vampires: Count Drogat has decided that the time for hiding in the shadows is over. He has revealed himself as an undead lord and instructed his vampire minions to cleanse his castle of the living. Your party is ambushed (r309) during the night as you sleep by 1D3 vampires (each †S7, E7*, W25). Vampires can only be harmed by magical weapons/spells or by a priest but are -1S if you have a priest in your party or if you have any foulbane plant (-2 if both). At the start of combat: vampires use mesmerism — you must roll less than or equal to W&W and each other character must roll 4–6 to be able to attack rather than being frozen in fear. If a vampire is ever reduced to less than half its Endurance: roll 1D6 at end of combat round — on 5–6 the vampire turns into a bat and flies away. In the confusion you may try to rob Drogat castle by rolling 1D6 — if less than W&W you manage to grab wealth 110. If you roll equal or higher: you must face another 1D3 vampires that attack you (r307). You can never return to Castle Drogat or enter the hex for the rest of the game.`},{tag:`e427`,title:`Lair of Lizard-men`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You see the wretched mud-covered lair of a hostile lizard-man clan. A group of 1D6+1 lizard-men (each S5, E5, W4) — huge reptiles covered in green scales — advance towards you. You can either surrender to them (go to e061), try to escape (r314) or attack (r306).`,note:`If you enter combat: each time a lizard-man inflicts at least 1 wound on a character roll 1D6 — on 1 one of those wounds is from the lizard-man's disease-ridden tongue and is a poisoned wound. Later visits: auto-captured unless roll less than W&W on 1D6. If you end the day at any surrounding hex: roll 1D6 after any normal travel event — on 1–2 you encounter a lizard-man patrol (options and stats as above).`},{tag:`e428`,title:`Fire Demon`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`Out of the darkness comes a huge and menacing shadow wreathed in flames and smoke — a fire demon from a hellish elemental plane. See r305. The demon (†S12, E10*) can only be hurt by magical weapons or spells from a wizard or magician.`,note:`Before combat: roll 1D6 for each other character in your party — on 1 that character is frozen in fear and cannot attack or join any escape (so they are killed if you escape the combat). Trying to escape in a combat round (r220e) also has a -1 penalty to the roll but you may escape if you sacrifice a wizard or magician to the demon. If you kill the fire demon: see e195 and roll twice for magical possessions.`},{tag:`e429`,title:`Danger in the Clouds`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You discover important events at the Hawk Lord's castle. Roll 1D6: on 1–4 see e429a; on 5–6 see e429b.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Castle Event (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–4`,`e429a Poisoned Roc: The Hawk Lord's pet roc has been poisoned by the arrow of a hunter and will die unless healed. If you use a Cure Poison Vial (e181): this will heal the roc. Alternatively if you have a witch or druid in your party: they may forage for healing herbs in any forest hex as a daily action (r203). Roll 1D6-3 for the number of herbs they collect each day — you will need 6 in total but must collect the herbs and return to the castle within the next week or it will be too late. If you cure the roc: the Hawk Lord is very grateful and will give you a tame roc (see e099) to join your party. You may not Seek an Audience (r211j) with the Hawk Lord until his roc is cured (and then gain +1 to roll)`],[`5–6`,`e429b Betrayal (Ψ): The Hawk Lord has betrayed you and standing in the courtyard of the castle is your sneering evil brother Volk (†S8, E8, W30) backed by a group of his mercenary royal guard (each S5, E4, W4). You may either fight (r305), try to escape (r314), escape flying (r313) or surrender (e061). If you kill Volk and his guards: you will have to escape the hex (r218) before the Hawk-men attack you. You can never return to this castle`]]}]},{tag:`e430`,title:`Beanstalk`,type:`event`,image:!0,desc:`You discover a huge beanstalk twisting upwards into the clouds. You may either ignore it or climb it.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Ignore Beanstalk (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–4`,`Event ends`],[`5–6`,`e118 – Giant`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Climb Beanstalk (1D6 per character with less than 5 Endurance)`,rows:[[`1`,`Character falls to their death`],[`Other`,`Character makes it to the top safely`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`At the Top of the Beanstalk (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e118 – Giant`],[`2`,`e118a – Giant's Castle`],[`3`,`e108 – Hawk-men Attack`],[`4`,`e117 – Castle in the Clouds`],[`5`,`e109 – Wild Pegasus`],[`6`,`e196 – Winged Boots and roll again here`]]}]},{tag:`e431`,title:`Waterfall`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`You discover a huge waterfall in front of you with water crashing down into a pool below. If you are rafting on the rivers (r213): you may go no further. If you are trying to cross a river: roll 1D6 — on 4+ you succeed. You may also explore behind the waterfall — roll 1D6 for any discovery.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Behind the Waterfall (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`A large empty cave — if you stay here ignore any events that take place after your evening meal (r215). You may also store a cache (r214) here without having to roll a die to find it again`],[`2`,`e068 – Wizard's Abode`],[`3`,`e064 – Ruins`],[`4`,`e166 – Elf Haven`],[`5`,`e045 – Arch of Travel`],[`6`,`e178 – Dryad`]]}]},{tag:`e432`,title:`Rats`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`A pack of 3D6 hungry scavenging rats (each S2, E2) attacks your party. They may surprise you (r308). Due to their small size a maximum of 6 rats can attack a character each round. Cannot escape from the rat attack but each time you kill a rat you automatically roll to see if they rout (r220f) with +1 to the roll.`,note:`If you are in a sewer: at the end of the combat each character that has taken wounds from the rat attack must roll 1D6 — on 1 one of their wounds is poisoned (r222a) due to infection carried by the rats.`},{tag:`e433`,title:`Unusual Neighbours`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You discover that this habitation has an unusual neighbouring habitation. Roll 1D6 for a random direction then 1D3+1 for the distance in hexes. Depending on the terrain there is another habitation in that hex — if you go there see the corresponding paragraph below.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Neighbouring Habitation by Terrain`,rows:[[`Countryside`,`e070 – Halfling Village`],[`Forest`,`e166 – Elf Haven`],[`Hills`,`e059 – Dwarf Mine`],[`Mountains`,`e118a – Giant's Castle`],[`Swamp`,`e068a – Wizard's Tower`],[`Desert`,`e064 – Ruins`]]}]},{tag:`e434`,title:`Graveyard`,type:`location`,image:!0,desc:`Postponed until after the evening meal (r215). You discover an old graveyard on the edge of the habitation and an eerie mist envelopes the graves and tombs there. Roll 1D6.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Graveyard Event (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e032 – Ghosts`],[`2`,`e170 – Vampire`],[`3`,`e400 – Hooded Figure`],[`4`,`e031 – Looted Tomb`],[`5`,`e038 – Cache under Stone`],[`6`,`A group of 1D3+1 flesh-eating ghouls are breaking into graves (each S5 / S4 with priest or monk, E5*, W0) — see r305. If you kill the ghouls: you see a broken chest in one of the disturbed graves — see e037`]]}]},{tag:`e435`,title:`Centaurs`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`A group of 1D3+1 centaurs (each S6, E6, W12) who are half man half horse emerge from the trees before you. Centaurs do not like men but not all are hostile. In combat (r220) centaurs automatically get a mounted bonus (r220h).`,note:`Add +1 to the roll if there is an elf in your party. Follow: centaurs bid you follow them deeper into the forest. If you don't: you must evade or fight. If you do follow: make a follow move (r219) to a random adjacent forest hex then roll 1D6 — 1–3→e198; 4–6→e053 (may peacefully share the campsite eating your own food r215, or you can fight the centaurs for control r305).`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6, +1 if elf in party)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`surprised r308`,`escape mtd r312`,`surprised r308`],[`2`,`attacked r306`,`pass r328`,`attacked r306`],[`3`,`pass r326`,`hide r317`,`attacked r306`],[`4`,`pass r325`,`escape r315`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`** follow`,`escape r311`,`attack r305`],[`6+`,`** follow`,`attacked r306`,`attack r304`]]}]},{tag:`e436`,title:`Bounty Hunter`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`(Ψ) You have encountered Tar Poth (†S9, E9, W30), the ruthless and feared half-orc bounty hunter from the Northlands. Poth has been sent after you by the usurpers with express orders to kill you on sight. In combat Poth can strike twice per round and always strikes against you personally for at least one of those strikes. Roll 1D6 — on 5–6 Poth is also mounted.`,note:`If your entire party has mounts but Poth is not mounted: add +1 to the evade roll. Subtract 1 if Poth is mounted and you are not. If your party all have winged mounts: can use a flying escape (r313) instead of rolling for the evade option. May abandon un-mounted members of your party in the attempt.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Evade*`,`Fight`],rows:[[`0–1`,`surprise r303`,`surprise r300`],[`2`,`attack r306`,`surprise r301`],[`3`,`attack r304`,`surprise r302`],[`4`,`hide r318`,`attack r304`],[`5`,`escape r311`,`attack r304`],[`6+`,`escape mounted r312`,`attack r305`]]}]},{tag:`e437`,title:`Giant Serpent`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`An enormous serpent at least forty feet in length rears up before you with huge fangs dripping with dark poison. The serpent is S7, E7 and is so quick that it has two strikes each combat round. Worse still all the wounds it inflicts are poisoned (r222a). See r330.`,note:`If you kill the serpent: you can use its venom to make a vial of a poison drug (see e185).`},{tag:`e438`,title:`Desert Nomads`,type:`encounter`,image:!0,desc:`You see a group of 1D3+1 tough desert nomad warriors approaching (each S5, E5, W4). Roll 1D6 — on 4–6 they also ride on camels (equivalent to a horse as a mount).`,note:`If the nomads join your party: they can act as guides (r205a) in any desert hex. They and any camel mounts will only need normal food requirement (r215a) in any desert hex as they carry and limit their own water. The nomads will not leave a desert hex however. If your party has mounts: may use escape mounted (r312) instead of rolling for an evade option.`,tables:[{type:`roll-table`,label:`Encounter Options (1D6)`,cols:[`Roll`,`Talk*`,`Evade**`,`Fight`],rows:[[`1`,`conversation r341`,`escape r311`,`surprise r302`],[`2`,`hirelings r338`,`escape r314`,`surprise r303`],[`3`,`hirelings r339`,`escape r315`,`attack r304`],[`4`,`looters r340`,`hide r319`,`attack r305`],[`5`,`pass r327`,`pass r328`,`attacked r306`],[`6`,`attacked r306`,`hide r317`,`battle r330`]]}]},{tag:`e439`,title:`Huge Nest`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You discover an enormous nest of branches and lichen and inside is a very large egg. Roll 1D6.`,note:`If the young creature joins your party: it is half the usual Skill and Endurance (round up) and is too small to function as a winged mount. You also find treasure worth W12 in the nest.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Nest Discovery (1D6)`,rows:[[`1–2`,`An angry mother arrives swooping down on you to protect the egg (r308). Roll 1D6 for the creature: 1–2→e101 (Harpies); 3–4→e099 (Roc); 5→e100 (Griffon); 6→e098 (Dragon). If you kill the mother creature: may investigate the nest and egg — see result 3–6 below`],[`3–6`,`You can choose to ignore the egg and pick up some small treasure (W12) or if you have a wizard/magician/witch/druid in your party: they can cast a spell on the egg to cause it to immediately hatch and grow (see 1–2 above to find what hatches). Roll 1D6: 1–5→it is very hungry and will join your party if you feed it 2 food units now (attacking if you cannot feed it); 6→it attacks you`]]}]},{tag:`e440`,title:`Celiana`,type:`story`,image:!0,desc:`(Ψ) You are surprised and delighted to encounter your twin sister Celiana and there is a joyful reunion for you both. She has been looking for you but bears bad news — the armies in the North are arming quicker than you thought and you only have nine weeks to complete your quest rather than ten!`,note:`Celiana (S4, E4, W21) joined an order of Northlands priests so may act as a priest in events. Roll 1D6 — on 4–6 she is also mounted. As your sister Celiana acts as if she were a true love (r228) with a +1 bonus to your W&W and the same rules if she is killed (r228a) but does not count in an eternal triangle.`},{tag:`e441`,title:`Rope Bridge`,type:`event`,image:!1,desc:`You see an old rope bridge ahead over a deep chasm with a river far below. It's the only way to cross the chasm and river for miles around. Unless your party can fly across (winged mounts etc): you'll either have to back track to the previous hex and then enter again to find another way to pass, or try to cross the bridge.`,note:`Each character must cross the bridge individually dismounting if necessary. You must decide the order in which they cross. Roll 2D6 for each character or mount that crosses — on a 2 (2–3 for a mount) the rope breaks, the bridge collapses and they fall into the chasm and are lost. Any characters on the opposite side of the chasm from you after the collapse are considered lost as well. If you are still on the original side of the bridge after the collapse: you will have to back track as above.`},{tag:`e442`,title:`Tax Collector`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`A mounted tax collector (S3, E3, W70) for the local lord rides up to you. He has 1D6 rough-looking guards (each S6, E5, W4) although these men are not mounted. The haughty tax collector sniffs at you and demands that you immediately pay 10% of your gold in taxes!`,note:`You can fight (r302) the collector, try to bribe him with 20 gold (r322) or give him the gold he demands. If you kill the tax collector: you will be wanted at the nearest town or castle. If the nearest habitation is a village however: you are something of a hero and always get free food (r215) and lodging (r217) there.`},{tag:`e443`,title:`Gorgon`,type:`combat`,image:!0,desc:`You have encountered a gorgon (†S5, E7, W30), a hideous fanged woman with writhing snakes for hair. The gaze of a gorgon will turn you to stone — immediately roll 1D6 for each character and mount in your party: on 1 (1–2 if a mount) they become a statue. Need not roll for any wizards/witches/magicians however as they will have magic that can block the gorgon's deadly power.`,note:`If you have a Shield of Light (e193): you bring the shield up and the gorgon sees her own reflection turning herself into stone. Must fight the gorgon (r306) but each character in your party is -2S as they try to avoid her gaze. At the end of each round: must check again for each character to see if they are turned to stone. If you kill the gorgon: any wizard/witch/magician can use a spell to bring stone characters back to life. Finally: roll 1D6 — on 4–6 the gorgon is guarding a magical altar (see e043).`},{tag:`e444`,title:`Beggar`,type:`encounter`,image:!1,desc:`You see an old beggar trying for a few coins. Decide on whether to give the beggar 3 gold then roll 1D6. If the result is 2–6 there is no further result apart from the beggar's gratitude but if the roll is 1 see below.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Gold Given — on roll of 1 (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e073 (friendly) – Witch`],[`2`,`e049 – Traveling Minstrel`],[`3`,`e069 – Wounded Warrior`],[`4`,`e425 – Disguised Noble`],[`5`,`e172 (alone) – Gypsy`],[`6`,`e173 – Thieves`]]},{type:`simple-list`,label:`Beggar Ignored (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e073 (hostile) – Witch`],[`2`,`The beggar curses you (see e018)`],[`3–4`,`Your followers are not impressed. Roll 2D6 for each follower then subtract your W&W — if the result is 4+ the character deserts you`],[`5`,`Your miserly attitude is witnessed by the local lord — if you have at least 20 gold you are arrested and taken to the nearest habitation (see e060, +2 to roll)`],[`6`,`The beggar attacks you (S3, E3, W0) — see r308 and he always attacks you personally`]]}]},{tag:`e445`,title:`Dark Wizard Cult`,type:`combat`,image:!1,desc:`(Ψ) Postponed until after your evening meal (r215). From the darkness appear the hooded figures of Dark Wizards allies to the usurpers of your throne. There are 1D3+1 wizards (each †S4, E4, W60) and with them are an equal number of terrifying golem servants (†S6, E8*, W0). See r330.`,note:`If the wizards have a surprise round (r220d): they try to take control of your party (see e024, rolling only once regardless of the number of wizards but with -1 to your W&W due to their dark magic). In any other combat round: the wizards may use fireball spells (see e023a, rolling for each wizard separately). If you win the fight and kill the wizards: the golems will immediately turn to clay. Breaking the power of their cult will also greatly improve the chances of you regaining your throne (Ψ) — you now need only 200 gold pieces to win the game. The dark wizards are also guarding a magical artefact — roll 1D6: 1→e041; 2→e042; 3→e043; 4→e045; 5→e046; 6→e047. If you escape the combat without killing the wizards: the cult will use their magic to track you. Each day after any normal events are concluded but before you take your evening meal (r215) roll 1D6: 1–2→e445 (only 1D3 wizards); 3→e002 (result 1); 4–5→you manage to avoid detection today but must roll again tomorrow; 6→you have eluded the cult for now and need not roll again here.`,tables:[{type:`simple-list`,label:`Magical Artefact Guarded (1D6)`,rows:[[`1`,`e041 – Vision Gem`],[`2`,`e042 – Alcove of Sending`],[`3`,`e043 – Small Altar`],[`4`,`e045 – Arch of Travel`],[`5`,`e046 – Gateway to Darkness`],[`6`,`e047 – Mirror of Reversal`]]}]}];export{e as t};